  { id:'opqb-t11-448', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-glauc',
    stem:'All the following associated open angle glaucoma include all the following **EXCEPT**:',
    options:["Roanne’s nasal step",'Enlarged blind spot','Generalized depression of isopters','Loss of central fields','Tubular vision'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**Central vision is the last thing glaucoma takes, not one of its characteristic field changes — so \"loss of central fields\" is the odd one out.**\n\n`L11) Glaucoma Diag, POAG` sets out the field changes twice, and both lists are peripheral. Slide 15 gives the full set: \"Paracentral scotomata in Bjerrum's area (10 – 20 degrees of central field) · Arcuate scotomata (superior, inferior) · Nasal step · Ring scotoma (double arcuate scotoma) · Constriction of visual field (→ tubular field) · Temporal island of vision\". The summary diagram on slides 3 and 22 repeats \"Bjerrum scotoma · Arcuate scotoma · Nasal step · Ring scotoma · Tubular field / temporal island\". Central acuity is listed separately and late — under *Visual acuity changes*, `L11` says only \"**Diminution of central vision in end stage disease**\".\n\nThat is the whole question. Central loss happens; it is not *characteristic*, because by the time it happens the diagnosis has been obvious for years.\n\n| Option | Verdict | Where the deck puts it |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A. Nasal step** | true change | `L11` slide 15, and again in the summary diagram |\n| **B. Enlarged blind spot** | true change | not in `L11`; see the note below |\n| **C. Generalized depression of isopters** | true change | `L11`'s \"Constriction of visual field\" is the same phenomenon named differently |\n| **D. Loss of central fields** | **NOT characteristic — the key** | `L11` confines it to \"end stage disease\" |\n| **E. Tubular vision** | true change | `L11` slide 15, \"(→ tubular field)\" |\n\n**On the eponym in option A.** The bank prints \"Roanne's nasal step\". The finding is Rønne's nasal step, named for the Danish ophthalmologist Henning Rønne. I checked the eponym against every cached source: `Roenne` and `Roanne` return **zero hits across all thirty lecture caches in `content\\ophtho\\lectures\\` and all twelve book chapter caches in `content\\ophtho\\book\\`**, case-insensitively. The *finding* is taught — `L11` prints \"Nasal step\" plainly — but the name attached to it is **not taken from the course material**. Recognise the phrase; do not expect the professor to use it.\n\n**On option B.** Blind-spot enlargement is not in `L11` either — the deck never mentions the blind spot in its glaucoma slides. It is a real glaucomatous change (the disc is where the axons die, so the scotoma that corresponds to the disc grows), and the bank treats it as true here, in `entep`-style fashion, and again in Q456 and Q468. That the enlarging blind spot is a glaucomatous field change is **not taken from the course material** in these decks, though `L21) Optic Nerve` and `L22) Pupil and Visual pathway` both discuss the blind spot in other contexts.\n\n**On option C.** \"Generalized depression of isopters\" is perimetrist's language for the whole field getting dimmer — every isopter contracting toward fixation. `L11` calls the end point of that process \"Constriction of visual field (→ tubular field)\". Same process, earlier stage.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Glaucomatous field loss is peripheral and paracentral — nasal step, arcuate and ring scotomata, generalised depression, tubular field; central vision is spared until end-stage disease, so loss of central field is not a characteristic change.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.71 (Q448), key p.77' },

  { id:'opqb-t11-449', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-glauc',
    stem:'The treatment of choice for the **other eye** in angle closure glaucoma is:',
    options:['Surgical peripheral iridectomy','Yag laser iridotomy','Trabeculotomy','Trabeculectomy'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"Standard **prophylactic** treatment for the fellow eye in angle-closure glaucoma is **a laser peripheral iridotomy.**\n\n---\n\n*(The line above is the bank's own printed explanation, transcribed verbatim. The notes below are added for study.)*\n\n**Why the fellow eye is treated at all.** `L12) Glaucoma 2 Cong, PACG, 2ry` lists \"**Affected fellow eye**\" among the patient characteristics that put an eye at risk of angle closure — the anatomy that closed one angle is bilateral, because it is the same crowded anterior segment in both orbits. The fellow eye is not a hypothetical risk; it is the same eye, one attack behind.\n\n**Why an iridotomy, and why laser.** `L12`'s pathogenesis chain for the acute attack runs: mid-dilated pupil → relative pupil block → aqueous accumulates behind the iris → iris bombé → the angle narrows → outflow resistance rises → IOP rises. An iridotomy puts a hole through the peripheral iris, so aqueous no longer has to squeeze past the pupil margin. That short-circuits the chain at its first link — which is why it is the *prophylactic* operation rather than a rescue one. `L12`'s surgical slide names it exactly: \"Iridotomy — **Laser (standard)**, Surgical\". The deck's own word for the laser route is *standard*.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong.**\n\n| Option | What it is | Why not |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A. Surgical peripheral iridectomy** | the same hole, cut with a blade in theatre | `L12` lists it, but as the alternative to laser, not the choice. An unaffected eye does not need an intraocular operation with its infection and hypotony risks when a slit-lamp laser does the same job in minutes. |\n| **C. Trabeculotomy** | opening Schlemm's canal from outside | `L12` gives it to **congenital** glaucoma with an opaque cornea, where the fault is a dysgenetic meshwork. It does nothing about pupil block. |\n| **D. Trabeculectomy** | a fistula from the anterior chamber to the subconjunctival space | `L11` and `L12` both list it, but as a filtering operation for eyes whose pressure is already uncontrolled. It is a large operation with a bleb to look after for life — indefensible in an eye that has never had an attack. |\n\n**A note on the spelling.** The bank prints \"Yag\"; the laser is a Nd:YAG (neodymium-doped yttrium aluminium garnet). `L12` says only \"Laser\" without naming the medium, so the specific laser type is **not taken from the course material**.",
    objective:'The fellow eye in primary angle closure shares the same crowded anterior segment, so it is treated prophylactically by laser peripheral iridotomy — which breaks the pupil-block mechanism before an attack, unlike trabeculotomy or trabeculectomy.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.71 (Q449), key p.77' },

  { id:'opqb-t11-450', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-glauc',
    stem:'Topical **atropine** is contraindicated in:',
    options:['Retinoscopy in children','Iridocyclitis','Corneal ulcer','Primary angle closure glaucoma'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**Atropine** dilates the pupil and is **contraindicated** in narrow angles as it can **precipitate an attack.**\n\n---\n\n*(The line above is the bank's own printed explanation, transcribed verbatim. The notes below are added for study.)*\n\n**The mechanism, in the deck's own chain.** `L12) Glaucoma 2 Cong, PACG, 2ry` opens its acute-attack pathway with a single line: \"**Mid-dilated position of the pupil**\" → relative pupil block → aqueous behind the iris → iris bombé → the angle narrows → IOP rises. The mid-dilated pupil is where the whole cascade starts, because that is the position in which the iris is both maximally apposed to the lens *and* maximally bunched into the angle. A mydriatic walks the pupil straight into it. `L12` also lists, among what *increases* the risk of angle closure, \"Use of systemic medications (**anticholinergics**)\" and \"Dark environment\" — two ways of arriving at the same pupil.\n\nAtropine is the longest-acting anticholinergic mydriatic in ophthalmic use, so it is the worst possible choice in a predisposed eye. Q459 asks the same fact from the other end and keys atropine as the drug that precipitates closed-angle glaucoma.\n\n**Why the other three are not contraindications — they are indications.**\n\n| Option | Why atropine is *used* |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Retinoscopy in children** | a child's accommodation is powerful enough to hide several dioptres of hypermetropia. Cycloplegia paralyses it so the true refraction can be measured. ⚠️ `L7,8) Refractive errors` does not name a single cycloplegic drug — its own coverage note records \"No cycloplegic agent is named anywhere in the deck — no atropine, no cyclopentolate, no tropicamide\" — so that atropine is the agent used for paediatric cycloplegic refraction is **not taken from the course material**. |\n| **B. Iridocyclitis** | `L13,14) Uvea & Sclera` lists \"Cycloplegic-mydriatic topical medications: to decrease photophobia and pain from iritis and ciliary spasm; to prevent and break recently formed posterior synechiae\". Atropine is a treatment here, not a hazard. |\n| **C. Corneal ulcer** | the same logic — a cyclitic, photophobic eye is rested by paralysing the ciliary muscle, and a wide pupil cannot form posterior synechiae behind an inflamed iris. |\n\n**The distinction worth carrying.** Mydriasis is dangerous only when the angle is already crowded. In an open angle it is a therapeutic or diagnostic manoeuvre performed thousands of times a day. Q465 makes the mirror-image point: in secondary glaucoma from acute iridocyclitis it is **pilocarpine**, the miotic, that must be avoided — because there the pupil must stay wide to keep the iris off the lens.",
    objective:'Atropine is contraindicated in primary angle closure glaucoma because mydriasis produces the mid-dilated pupil that starts pupil block; in iridocyclitis, corneal ulcer and paediatric retinoscopy the same drug is an indication.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.71 (Q450), key p.77' },

  { id:'opqb-t11-451', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-glauc',
    stem:'**Neovascular glaucoma** follows:',
    options:['Thrombosis of central retinal vein','Acute congestive glaucoma','Staphylococcal infection','Hypertension'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**Neovascular glaucoma follows retinal ischaemia, and the commonest ischaemic event that produces it is an ischaemic central retinal vein occlusion.**\n\n`L12) Glaucoma 2 Cong, PACG, 2ry` files neovascular glaucoma under *secondary narrow angle glaucoma* and gives it a mechanism: \"Neovascular membrane grows over the trabecular meshwork and contracts pulling the peripheral iris over the trabeculum\". Two examples down the same list is \"**Retinal Vein Occlusion**\", and `L19.2) Retinal Vascular Disorders` supplies the missing arithmetic: ischaemic CRVO shows \"**Rubeosis irides in 50%**\", and its management is to \"Check every month for 6 months · Look for rubeosis and angle new vessels\".\n\nSo the chain is: extensive capillary non-perfusion → the ischaemic retina releases angiogenic factor → new vessels grow on the iris (rubeosis) and into the angle → the fibrovascular membrane contracts → the angle is pulled shut → the pressure rises. `L12` calls the mechanism \"**anterior pulling**\", as opposed to the \"posterior pushing\" of a tumour or a swollen lens.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong.**\n\n| Option | What it actually is |\n|---|---|\n| **B. Acute congestive glaucoma** | the old name for an acute angle closure crisis. This is a *primary* angle closure with a pupil-block mechanism — `L12`'s own chain — and it has no neovascular component at all. It does not cause NVG; it is a different disease that also closes an angle. |\n| **C. Staphylococcal infection** | causes lid and ocular surface disease and endophthalmitis. Neither produces iris neovascularisation. |\n| **D. Hypertension** | systemic hypertension damages retinal arterioles and is a risk factor for vein occlusion, but it does not itself produce the widespread capillary closure that drives rubeosis. It is the cause of the cause, one step too far back. |\n\n**The other ischaemic causes.** `L12` lists retinal vein occlusion and intraocular tumours; Q458 in this same topic adds proliferative diabetic retinopathy and asks which vascular event is *least* likely (branch retinal artery occlusion — because it starves only a wedge of retina). Q454 is the same disease under a nickname. Together the four questions are one table: **NVG follows whatever leaves a large area of retina without blood.**\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Neovascular glaucoma follows widespread retinal ischaemia — classically ischaemic central retinal vein occlusion, in which rubeosis appears in about half of eyes — and closes the angle by anterior pulling of a fibrovascular membrane.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.71 (Q451), key p.77' },

  { id:'opqb-t11-452', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-glauc',
    stem:'A **one-month-old baby** is brought with complaints of **photophobia and watering**. Clinical examinations show **normal tear passages** and **clear but large cornea**. The most likely diagnosis is:',
    options:['Congenital dacryocystitis','Interstitial keratitis','Keratoconus','Buphthalmos'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"Large cornea in a 1-month-old with photophobia and tearing → **congenital glaucoma (buphthalmos)**\n\n---\n\n*(The line above is the bank's own printed explanation, transcribed verbatim. The notes below are added for study.)*\n\n**The triad in the stem is `L12`'s symptom list, word for word.** `L12) Glaucoma 2 Cong, PACG, 2ry` gives congenital glaucoma these symptoms: \"**Photophobia, watering, blepharospasm** · Large eye · Abnormal colour of the eye · Diminution of vision\", and these signs: \"Corneal oedema, Haab's stria · **Enlarged corneal diameter** · Broad limbus · Increased IOP · Optic nerve cupping · Increased axial length (A-scan)\". The stem names two of the three symptoms and the cardinal sign.\n\n**Why a baby's eye enlarges and an adult's does not.** `L12`'s pathogenesis has two halves. The primary fault is at the angle — \"Persistent mesodermal membrane on trabecular meshwork (goniodysgenesis) · Anterior iris insertion obscuring trabecular meshwork · Absent Schlemm's canal\". The secondary effects follow from the raised pressure acting on a coat that is still distensible: \"enlargement of the globe → increased corneal diameter, broad limbus, increased antero-posterior diameter of the eye · aqueous entry into corneal stroma → corneal oedema · optic nerve cupping\". An adult sclera does not stretch, so an adult with the same pressure gets cupping and nothing else.\n\n**Why \"normal tear passages\" is in the stem.** It is there to kill option A. Congenital nasolacrimal duct obstruction is the commonest cause of a watering infant eye by a wide margin, and `L12` lists it explicitly in the differential diagnosis of congenital glaucoma. Patent passages remove it, and what remains that waters is a photophobic cornea.\n\n**Why the other two are wrong.**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **B. Interstitial keratitis** | inflames and clouds the corneal stroma. The stem says the cornea is **clear**, and IK is a disease of later childhood, typically congenital syphilis — not a one-month-old. |\n| **C. Keratoconus** | ectasia that steepens and thins the cornea; it presents in the teens and twenties with progressive irregular astigmatism, and it does not enlarge the corneal *diameter*. `L15,16) Cornea` puts it well outside infancy. |\n\n**The trap in \"clear cornea\".** Corneal oedema is on `L12`'s sign list, so a clear cornea might look like an argument against glaucoma. It is not — it is a statement about how early the child has presented, and `L12` makes it clinically useful: with a **clear cornea the operation is goniotomy**; with an opaque one it is trabeculotomy, trabeculectomy with antimetabolites, or a valve. Q460 in this same topic keys goniotomy on exactly that line.",
    objective:'Photophobia, watering and an enlarged clear cornea in an infant with patent tear passages is buphthalmos (congenital glaucoma) — the infant sclera stretches under raised pressure, which is why the globe enlarges.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.71 (Q452), key p.77' },

  { id:'opqb-t11-453', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-glauc',
    stem:'You have been referred to a case of **open angle glaucoma**. Which of the following would be an important point in **diagnosing** the case?',
    options:['Shallow anterior chamber','Optic disc cupping','Narrow angle','Visual acuity and refractive error'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**Glaucoma is diagnosed at the optic nerve head, and in an OPEN angle the disc is the only positive sign on the list.**\n\n`L11) Glaucoma Diag, POAG` defines the disease twice, and the disc is in both. The triad: \"Elevated intraocular pressure (IOP) · **Optic nerve affection (cupping)** · Visual field defects\". The modern restatement: \"Glaucoma is **a specific type of optic neuropathy** in which intraocular pressure (IOP) is a significant risk factor\" — with the deck spelling out that IOP is a risk factor rather than the disease, since \"eyes with optic neuropathy and normal IOP (normal tension glaucoma)\" and \"eyes with normal optic nerves and elevated IOP (ocular hypertension)\" both exist.\n\nThe structural change `L11` asks you to find is \"Neural rim thinning\" — \"Focal → polar notch → superior, inferior\" or \"Circumferential → generalized thinning of the neural rim\" — and \"Clinically → **enlargement of cup/disc ratio > 0.3**\", with splinter haemorrhage and total cupping with nasal shift of the vessels as additional features.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong — and two of them are wrong for the same reason.**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Shallow anterior chamber** | a feature of the *closed* angle eye. `L12` lists it among the signs of acute angle closure and in the risk profile (\"crowded anterior segments\"). In open angle glaucoma the chamber is normal. |\n| **C. Narrow angle** | contradicts the stem outright. `L11` defines an open angle gonioscopically as one where \"outflow route (trabecular meshwork) **visibly exposed** to the aqueous humour\". If the angle were narrow it would not be open angle glaucoma. |\n| **D. Visual acuity and refractive error** | acuity is normal until the end. `L11` says the presentation is \"Most commonly → **asymptomatic**\", with visual loss only in \"Advanced disease\". Refraction is a *predisposing factor* — the deck lists \"Refractive state: more common in **myopia**\" — but a risk factor is not a diagnostic finding. |\n\n**On the threshold in `L11`.** The deck's cup/disc cut-off of **0.3** is one of three figures in circulation in this module: the book prints a normal range of 0.2–0.4 with referral above 0.5, and the shipped `pup-10` chapter prints 0.3–0.5 with referral above 0.6. All three are held, none is corrected. Nothing in this question turns on which you use — the point is that the *disc* is where the diagnosis is made, not what number you attach to it.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Open angle glaucoma is an optic neuropathy, so the diagnostic finding is neuroretinal rim thinning and cupping at the disc — a shallow chamber and a narrow angle belong to angle closure, and acuity is normal until end-stage disease.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.71 (Q453), key p.77' },

  { id:'opqb-t11-454', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-glauc',
    stem:"**100 days’ glaucoma** is seen in:",
    options:['Central Retinal Artery Occlusion','Branch Retinal Artery Occlusion','Central Retinal Vein Occlusion','Branch Retinal Vein Occlusion'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**100-day glaucoma refers to** neovascular glaucoma occurring **~3 months after ischemic CRVO.**\n\n---\n\n*(The line above is the bank's own printed explanation, transcribed verbatim. The notes below are added for study.)*\n\n**On the name.** I checked it against every cached source: `100 day` and `100-day` return **zero hits across all thirty lecture caches and all twelve book chapter caches**, case-insensitively. The nickname is **not taken from the course material** — and neither is the ~3-month interval the bank gives for it. What *is* taught is everything the nickname compresses.\n\n**What the decks do give.** `L19.2) Retinal Vascular Disorders` describes ischaemic CRVO as a disease of \"Extensive capillary non-perfusion\", with \"**Rubeosis irides in 50%**\", \"Very poor prognosis\", and a management protocol of \"**Check every month for 6 months** · Look for rubeosis and angle new vessels · Treat neovascularization by panretinal photocoagulation\". `L12) Glaucoma 2 Cong, PACG, 2ry` closes the loop by listing \"**Retinal Vein Occlusion**\" as a cause of secondary narrow angle glaucoma. Put together, the six-month watch exists precisely because the rubeosis arrives on a delay — which is what the nickname is about.\n\n**Why a vein and not an artery.**\n\n| Option | What the occlusion does |\n|---|---|\n| **A. CRAO** | infarcts the inner retina in minutes. `L19.2` gives it arteriolar and venular narrowing and a cherry-red spot. The retina dies rather than surviving in a hypoxic state, so it is a far less reliable producer of angiogenic factor. Rubeosis after CRAO is described but uncommon. |\n| **B. BRAO** | starves one wedge only. Too little retina is involved to drive iris neovascularisation. Q458 keys branch retinal artery occlusion as the *least* likely cause of NVG for exactly this reason. |\n| **C. CRVO** | **the key.** Blood stagnates in a still-perfused but hypoxic retina — the state that produces angiogenic factor most reliably, and over the whole fundus. |\n| **D. BRVO** | the same mechanism as CRVO but confined to one quadrant. It can neovascularise, but usually at the disc or retina rather than the iris, and it does not carry the 50 % rubeosis figure `L19.2` attaches to the central form. |\n\n**The clinical point that survives the nickname.** An ischaemic CRVO is not finished when the haemorrhages fade. `L19.2`'s monthly review for six months exists so that the angle is examined before the pressure rises, because once the fibrovascular membrane has contracted across the trabeculum — `L12`'s \"anterior pulling\" — the glaucoma is very hard to reverse.",
    objective:'“100-day glaucoma” is neovascular glaucoma appearing roughly three months after an ischaemic central retinal vein occlusion, which is why such eyes are reviewed monthly for six months looking for rubeosis and angle new vessels.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.71 (Q454), key p.77' },

  { id:'opqb-t11-455', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-glauc',
    stem:'The method of examination of **anterior chamber angle** is called.',
    options:['Ophthalmoscopy','Retinoscopy','Gonioscopy','Laparoscopy'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**Gonioscopy.** `L11) Glaucoma Diag, POAG` defines it in one line — \"**Gonioscopy → visualization of the angle of the anterior chamber**\" — and `L12` repeats the same sentence when it introduces angle closure.\n\n**Why a special technique is needed at all.** The angle cannot be seen directly from in front. Light leaving it strikes the corneal front surface beyond the critical angle and is totally internally reflected, so the recess never emerges from the eye. A goniolens is placed on the cornea to defeat that: it replaces the cornea–air interface with a cornea–lens interface of similar refractive index, and a mirror inside the lens then turns the view through roughly a right angle so the observer can look across the chamber. *(The total-internal-reflection explanation is standard optics and is **not taken from the course material**; the decks state what gonioscopy is, not why it is optically necessary.)*\n\n**What it decides.** Both glaucoma decks make gonioscopy the classifying investigation, not an optional extra:\n\n> \"Open angle → outflow route (trabecular meshwork) **visibly exposed** to the aqueous humour. Closed angle → outflow route (trabecular meshwork) **not visibly exposed** to the aqueous humour.\" — `L11`, and again in `L12`\n\n`L1,2) Ocular Anatomy, Symptomatology & Basic Eye Examination` shows the view itself on slides 22–24, with the mnemonic \"I CAN'T SEE THE STUPID LINE\" running outwards from the **I**ris through the **C**iliary body, **S**cleral spur, **T**rabecular meshwork to **S**chwalbe's **L**ine, and a further photograph captioned \"Open Angle ⇢ Narrow Angle ⇢ Closed Angle\".\n\n**Why the other three are wrong.**\n\n| Option | What it examines |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Ophthalmoscopy** | the fundus — disc, macula, vessels and peripheral retina. The posterior segment, not the angle. |\n| **B. Retinoscopy** | refraction. An objective measurement of the eye's refractive error from the movement of a reflex in the pupil. |\n| **D. Laparoscopy** | the abdominal cavity. Not an ophthalmic procedure at all — the distractor is there to be discarded. |\n\nQ467 in this same topic asks what gonioscopy *shows*, and Q479 shows a photograph of the lens itself. Q464, Q476 and Q493 all turn on the same clinical fact: the angle has to be looked at before the operation is chosen.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Gonioscopy is the examination of the anterior chamber angle, and it is what classifies a glaucoma as open or closed angle — the angle cannot be seen without a goniolens because of total internal reflection at the cornea.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.71 (Q455), key p.77' },

  { id:'opqb-t11-456', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-glauc',
    stem:'Which statement of the following is **WRONG**:',
    options:['Only central field is important in glaucoma','Peripheral field changes occur in glaucoma','Enlargement of blind spot is glaucomatous field change','Tubular fields can occur in glaucoma'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**Glaucoma** initially affects **peripheral fields,** not just central.\n\n---\n\n*(The line above is the bank's own printed explanation, transcribed verbatim. The notes below are added for study.)*\n\n**This is Q448 turned inside out**, and the same slide answers both. `L11) Glaucoma Diag, POAG` lists the field changes of the disease as \"Paracentral scotomata in Bjerrum's area (10 – 20 degrees of central field) · Arcuate scotomata (superior, inferior) · **Nasal step** · Ring scotoma (double arcuate scotoma) · **Constriction of visual field (→ tubular field)** · **Temporal island of vision**\", and confines central involvement to one line under visual acuity: \"Diminution of central vision **in end stage disease**\".\n\nOption A is therefore wrong twice over. It is wrong about which field matters — the nasal step, the arcuate scotoma and the constricting periphery are all outside the central 20° — and it is wrong about what a normal central field proves, because a patient can read 6/6 with a field reduced to a tube.\n\n**Why the other three are right.**\n\n| Option | Where `L11` supports it |\n|---|---|\n| **B. Peripheral field changes occur** | the nasal step, ring scotoma, constriction and temporal island are all peripheral. |\n| **C. Enlargement of the blind spot** | ⚠️ this one is not in `L11`, which never mentions the blind spot in its glaucoma slides. It is a real change — the disc is where the axons are lost, so the scotoma that maps to it grows — and the bank treats it as true here and lists it as true again in Q448. That it is a glaucomatous change is **not taken from these glaucoma decks**; `L21) Optic Nerve` and `L22) Pupil and Visual pathway` are where the blind spot appears in this module. |\n| **D. Tubular fields can occur** | `L11` prints \"Constriction of visual field (→ tubular field)\" and repeats \"Tubular field / temporal island\" in its summary diagram. |\n\n**The clinical weight of getting this right.** `L11` says the presentation is \"Most commonly → **asymptomatic**\", and this is why: the patient's central vision, which is the vision they are aware of, stays intact while the periphery is eaten away. A glaucoma clinic that measured only acuity would discharge every early case. Q477, Q492 and Q497 all rest on the same point — the field, not the acuity and not the pressure, is what tells you the disease is moving.",
    objective:'Glaucoma affects the peripheral and paracentral field first and spares central vision until end-stage disease, so “only the central field matters” is false — perimetry, not acuity, detects the disease.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.71 (Q456), key p.77' },

  { id:'opqb-t11-457', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-glauc',
    stem:'Which of the following drugs does **NOT decrease aqueous formation**?',
    options:['Beta blockers','Prostaglandin analogues','Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors','Alpha adrenergic agonists'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**Prostaglandins** **increase uveoscleral outflow,** not reduce aqueous formation.\n\n---\n\n*(The line above is the bank's own printed explanation, transcribed verbatim. The notes below are added for study.)*\n\n**`L11) Glaucoma Diag, POAG` prints the whole table in two lines, and this question is one of them.** The deck's medical treatment slide reads:\n\n> \"Medical treatment → **Decreasing aqueous humour production** → beta-blockers, carbonic anhydrase inhibitors, alpha agonists. **Increasing aqueous humour drainage** → prostaglandin analogues, miotics, alpha agonists.\"\n\nThat is the answer verbatim: three of the four options are on the production-lowering line, and prostaglandin analogues are the only one that is not.\n\n**The two-column table, since three questions in this topic ask about it.**\n\n| Class | Reduces production | Increases drainage | `L11` |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Beta blockers** | ✔ | — | production line |\n| **Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors** | ✔ | — | production line |\n| **Alpha agonists** | ✔ | ✔ | ⚠️ `L11` lists them on **both** lines |\n| **Prostaglandin analogues** | — | ✔ | drainage line |\n| **Miotics (pilocarpine)** | — | ✔ | drainage line |\n\nThis is the shared table for **Q457, Q469 and Q484** in this topic; the sibling entries point back here rather than repeating it.\n\n**On the alpha agonists.** `L11` really does print them twice — under production *and* under drainage — and that is not a transcription slip. Q457's key survives it anyway: whichever way alpha agonists are read, they *do* reduce production, so option D is not the exception. Q469 exploits the same duplication from the other side.\n\n**Where the aqueous comes from and goes.** `L11`'s physiology slide: \"Aqueous humour production → ciliary processes around lens equator. Aqueous humour circulation → posterior chamber → pupil → anterior chamber → angle of anterior chamber → drainage. Aqueous humour drainage → trabecular meshwork → Schlemm's canal → collector channels → systemic circulation.\" A production-lowering drug acts at the first step, at the ciliary epithelium; a drainage drug acts at the last. Prostaglandin analogues act on neither of those routes exactly — they open the *uveoscleral* pathway, a secondary outflow through the ciliary muscle bundles into the suprachoroidal space. `L11` names the drainage route only as the trabecular one, so **the uveoscleral pathway itself is not taken from the course material**, though the bank's own box names it.",
    objective:'Beta blockers, carbonic anhydrase inhibitors and alpha agonists lower aqueous production; prostaglandin analogues do not — they lower pressure by increasing outflow, chiefly by the uveoscleral route.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.72 (Q457), key p.77' },

  { id:'opqb-t11-458', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-glauc',
    stem:'**Neovascular glaucoma** is **least likely** to occur as a result of this.',
    options:['Central retinal vein occlusion','Central Retinal Artery Occlusion','Branch retinal artery occlusion','Proliferative diabetic retinopathy'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**Neovascular glaucoma needs a large area of ischaemic retina, and a branch retinal artery occlusion starves the smallest area on the list.**\n\nThe mechanism is `L12) Glaucoma 2 Cong, PACG, 2ry`'s: \"Neovascular membrane grows over the trabecular meshwork and contracts pulling the peripheral iris over the trabeculum\", listed under secondary **narrow** angle glaucoma with \"anterior pulling\" as its mechanism. What drives the membrane is hypoxic retina — and how much of it there is decides whether the iris neovascularises at all.\n\n| Option | Area of retina made ischaemic | Likelihood of NVG |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A. CRVO** | the whole fundus. `L19.2) Retinal Vascular Disorders` gives ischaemic CRVO \"Extensive capillary non-perfusion\" and \"**Rubeosis irides in 50 %**\" | highest — this is the classic cause, and Q451 and Q454 both key it |\n| **B. CRAO** | the whole inner retina, but infarcted rather than surviving hypoxic | possible, less common than CRVO |\n| **C. BRAO** | **one wedge only, distal to a single arteriolar branch — the key** | least likely |\n| **D. Proliferative diabetic retinopathy** | widespread capillary closure by definition — proliferation is the *proof* that angiogenic factor is being made | high; `L12` lists intraocular new vessels among the anterior-pulling causes and `L19.1` gives PDR its own treatment by panretinal photocoagulation |\n\n**Why the arterial/venous split is not the discriminator.** It is tempting to answer \"arteries don't cause it, veins do\" and pick B. That over-simplifies: the real variable is *how much* retina is left alive and starving. A central artery occlusion does involve the whole retina — it is on the list of recognised, if uncommon, causes. A **branch** artery occlusion is disqualified by area, not by being arterial. The stem says *least likely*, and between an event affecting the entire retina and one affecting a sector, the sector loses.\n\n**What the treatment implies.** `L19.2`'s instruction for ischaemic CRVO is to \"Treat neovascularization by **panretinal** photocoagulation\" — pan-retinal, because the whole ischaemic retina has to be ablated to stop the angiogenic drive. That the therapy is proportional to area is itself the argument for this key.\n\n⚠️ **The relative frequency of NVG after CRAO versus BRAO is not stated in the decks.** `L19.2` never lists rubeosis under either arterial occlusion. The ranking above is reasoned from the shared mechanism and is **not taken from the course material**.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Neovascular glaucoma is driven by the area of ischaemic retina, so a branch retinal artery occlusion — a single sector — is the least likely cause, well behind ischaemic CRVO and proliferative diabetic retinopathy.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.72 (Q458), key p.77' },

  { id:'opqb-t11-459', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-glauc',
    stem:'**Closed angle glaucoma** can occur in predisposed eyes with the use of:',
    options:['Atropine.','Cortisone.','Vitamin A','Vitamin E.'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**Atropine dilates the pupil, and a mid-dilated pupil is the first link in `L12`'s chain.**\n\n`L12) Glaucoma 2 Cong, PACG, 2ry` builds the acute attack from one starting condition: \"**Mid-dilated position of the pupil** → relative pupil block → accumulation of aqueous humour behind the iris → iris bombe → narrowing of the angle of the anterior chamber → increased resistance to aqueous humour outflow → accumulation of aqueous humour in the eye → elevation of IOP (Rapid severe rise of IOP)\". Beside it the deck lists what *increases* the risk in an already crowded eye: \"Use of systemic medications (**anticholinergics**)\" and \"Dark environment\".\n\nAtropine is the archetypal anticholinergic mydriatic. In a predisposed eye — `L12`'s own list is \"Females · **Hyperopes** · Race (South-East Asians) · Advancing age · Affected fellow eye\" — it walks the pupil into the one position that maximises both iris–lens apposition and peripheral iris crowding.\n\n**Why the mid position and not full dilatation.** This is worth holding onto, because it is counter-intuitive. A widely dilated pupil pulls the iris *taut* and away from the lens, reducing pupil block; a constricted pupil holds the iris flat. It is the intermediate position that both presses the iris against the lens over a broad ring and bunches the peripheral iris into the recess. That is why an attack classically begins in the dark, or in a cinema, or after a dilating drop wears partly off.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong.**\n\n| Option | Effect on IOP |\n|---|---|\n| **B. Cortisone** | steroids do raise pressure — but by a completely different route and into an **open** angle. `L11) Glaucoma Diag, POAG` lists \"**Steroid induced**\" under secondary *open* angle glaucoma: \"Prolonged intake of potent steroids in a predisposed individual → decreased permeability of the trabecular meshwork to aqueous humour\". The meshwork clogs; the angle stays open. The stem specifies closed angle, so this is the near-miss distractor. |\n| **C. Vitamin A** | hypervitaminosis A can raise intracranial pressure and cause papilloedema; it has no effect on the angle. |\n| **D. Vitamin E** | no established effect on intraocular pressure at all. |\n\nQ450 asks the same fact as a contraindication and keys atropine there too. The pair is worth reading together: **atropine is forbidden in the narrow angle and prescribed in the inflamed one** — Q465 keys pilocarpine, not atropine, as the drug to avoid in glaucoma secondary to iridocyclitis.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Anticholinergic mydriatics such as atropine precipitate angle closure in a predisposed eye by producing the mid-dilated pupil that starts relative pupil block; steroids also raise pressure but through the trabecular meshwork, in an open angle.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.72 (Q459), key p.77' },

  { id:'opqb-t11-460', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-glauc',
    stem:'The best line of treatment for **congenital glaucoma** is:',
    options:['Pilocarpine eye drops','Peripheral iridectomy','Betablocker eye drops','Goniotomy'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**Congenital glaucoma is a surgical disease, and with a clear cornea the operation is goniotomy.**\n\n`L12) Glaucoma 2 Cong, PACG, 2ry` is unambiguous. Treatment is split into two lines: \"**Medical** → **Temporary till surgery**\" and \"**Surgical**\". The surgical line then divides on the cornea: \"**Clear cornea → goniotomy**\"; \"**Opaque cornea →** Trabeculotomy · Trabeculectomy with antimetabolites · Synthetic valve implantation\".\n\n**Why surgery and not drops.** The fault is structural and congenital. `L12`'s pathology is \"Persistent mesodermal membrane on trabecular meshwork (goniodysgenesis) · Anterior iris insertion obscuring trabecular meshwork · **Absent Schlemm's canal**\". No topical agent can remove a membrane or build a canal; a drop can only buy time while the child is prepared for theatre — which is exactly what the deck means by \"temporary till surgery\".\n\n**What goniotomy does.** An incision is made across the abnormal trabecular tissue from *inside* the eye, under direct gonioscopic view. `L12`'s own illustration for the step is captioned only \"Lens\" and \"Knife\", showing the blade passed across the chamber. Its prerequisite is written into the deck's rule: you must be able to *see* the angle to cut it, so the cornea has to be clear. When corneal oedema has already clouded the view, the surgeon works from outside instead — trabeculotomy — which is why the deck lists that as the opaque-cornea alternative.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong.**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Pilocarpine eye drops** | a miotic that increases trabecular drainage in an eye whose meshwork works. Here the meshwork is covered by a mesodermal membrane and Schlemm's canal may be absent — there is nothing to drain into. |\n| **B. Peripheral iridectomy** | relieves **pupil block**, which is the mechanism of *angle closure*, not of goniodysgenesis. The infant angle is not closed by an iris bombé; it is malformed. |\n| **C. Betablocker eye drops** | reduces aqueous production, so it lowers pressure — and it is a legitimate holding measure under `L12`'s \"temporary till surgery\". But it does nothing to the obstruction, and a globe that is still enlarging needs the obstruction dealt with. |\n\n**The stakes.** `L12` records that the disease \"Affects males in 65 % of cases, and is **bilateral in 70 %**\", and is \"common\" in communities with high rates of consanguineous marriage while remaining rare (1 in 10,000 live births) in Western ones. Two eyes, an infant visual system, and a globe that stretches under pressure — the reason the deck makes medicine temporary is that delay costs both the optic nerve and the developing fixation reflex. Q452 and Q494 are the diagnostic halves of the same disease.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Congenital glaucoma is treated surgically — goniotomy where the cornea is clear enough to see the angle, trabeculotomy or a filtering/valve procedure where it is not; drops are only a holding measure before theatre.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.72 (Q460), key p.77' },

  { id:'opqb-t11-461', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-glauc',
    stem:'Causes of **secondary glaucoma** do **NOT** include:',
    options:['Corneal ulcer','Iridocyclitis','Mature senile cataract','Central retinal vein thrombosis'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**Three of these four are named as causes of secondary glaucoma in the decks; the corneal ulcer is not.**\n\n\"Secondary\" has a precise meaning in both glaucoma decks: `L11) Glaucoma Diag, POAG` defines secondary open angle glaucoma as one where \"**A cause is seen for the increased resistance of aqueous humour outflow**\", and `L12` mirrors it for the narrow angle — \"A cause is seen for the gonioscopically narrow angle\". The question is therefore whether each option puts something in the outflow route or pulls the angle shut.\n\n| Option | Named where | Mechanism |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A. Corneal ulcer** | **nowhere on either deck's list — the key** | an ulcer sits on the corneal surface, outside the aqueous outflow route entirely. It does not obstruct the meshwork and does not close the angle. |\n| **B. Iridocyclitis** | `L11`, under secondary **open** angle | \"Deposition of inflammatory cells and plasmoid (viscid) aqueous humour → decreased permeability of the trabecular meshwork\". `L13,14) Uvea & Sclera` independently lists \"Secondary glaucoma\" among the complications of anterior uveitis. |\n| **C. Mature senile cataract** | `L17) Cataract I` and `L18) Cataract II` | lens-induced glaucoma, both types: **phacomorphic** — \"Intumescence (lens fibres fluid imbibition) → increased lens thickness → pupil block → elevated IOP\" — and **phacolytic** — \"Degeneration of lens capsule → leakage of lens proteins → … clogging of TM by lens proteins macromolecules → secondary open angle glaucoma\". |\n| **D. Central retinal vein thrombosis** | `L12`, under secondary **narrow** angle | listed twice over: as \"**Retinal Vein Occlusion** — congestion of the retinal veins causes forward rotation of the iris lens diaphragm\", and through neovascular glaucoma, whose membrane \"grows over the trabecular meshwork and contracts pulling the peripheral iris over the trabeculum\". |\n\n**The one caveat worth stating.** A severe corneal ulcer *can* end in raised pressure — if it perforates and the iris plugs the wound, or if the accompanying inflammation is severe enough to become the iridocyclitis of option B. But then the glaucoma is secondary to the inflammation or to the anatomical distortion, not to the ulceration as such, and **neither glaucoma deck lists corneal ulcer as a cause**. I checked: `corneal ulcer` appears in `L15,16) Cornea` and in `L12`'s *differential diagnosis of congenital glaucoma*, never in a list of secondary glaucoma causes. Option A is the answer the bank wants and the answer the decks support.\n\n**The full secondary list from `L11`, worth memorising as a block:** pseudoexfoliation syndrome (\"Pseudoexfoliative material … deposited in the trabecular meshwork\"), pigment dispersion syndrome (\"Pigment from the iris is chaffed out and deposited in the trabecular meshwork\"), steroid induced, and uveitic.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Secondary glaucoma means a visible cause for outflow resistance or angle narrowing — uveitis, a mature cataract and retinal vein occlusion all qualify, whereas a corneal ulcer lies outside the outflow route.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.72 (Q461), key p.77' },

  { id:'opqb-t11-462', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-glauc',
    stem:'**Field changes in POAG**:',
    options:['Enlargement Blind Spot','Arcuate scotoma','Centrocaecal','Altitudinal'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"Specific field changes POAG:\n\n1. **Paracentral scotomas:** multiple isolated scotomata in the zone between 10 and 20 degrees from the fixation point. [The paracentral area is the first to be affected in POAG]\n2. **Arcuate scotoma** that arches above and below the fixation point.\n3. **Nasal step:** Due to asymmetric involvement of the arcuate nerve bundles above and below the fixation point.\n4. **Tubular field (Tunnel vision):** The field of vision may constrict until it is limited to a few degrees around fixation. Fixation (central vision) may be spared until the disease is markedly advanced.\n5. **End-stage glaucoma;** These residual seeing areas may be eventually lost leaving the patient blind\n\n---\n\n*(The five numbered points above are the bank's own printed explanation, transcribed verbatim. The notes below are added for study.)*\n\n**The box is `L11) Glaucoma Diag, POAG` almost word for word.** The deck's own list is \"Paracentral scotomata in **Bjerrum's area (10 – 20 degrees of central field)** · Arcuate scotomata (superior, inferior) · Nasal step · Ring scotoma (double arcuate scotoma) · Constriction of visual field (→ tubular field) · Temporal island of vision\", with visual acuity handled separately as \"Diminution of central vision in end stage disease\". The bank's box adds nothing and omits only the ring scotoma and the temporal island.\n\n**This is the shared field table for the topic.** Q448, Q456 and Q468 all draw on it; those entries point here rather than reprinting it.\n\n**Why the anatomy makes these shapes and not others.** Retinal nerve fibres arch *around* the macula rather than crossing it, entering the disc above and below the horizontal raphe. Glaucoma kills them bundle by bundle at the rim, so the scotoma inherits the arc of the bundle — hence the arcuate shape — and stops abruptly at the horizontal midline, because fibres from above and below never mix. That step at the horizontal, seen nasally where the two arcs meet, is the nasal step, which is why the box explains it as \"asymmetric involvement of the arcuate nerve bundles above and below\". *(The retinal raphe and the arcuate course of the nerve fibre layer are anatomical background; `L11` gives the field shapes and the asymmetry, not the fibre anatomy, so this paragraph is **not taken from the course material**.)*\n\n**Why the other three are wrong — each belongs to a different disease.**\n\n| Option | Whose defect it is |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Enlargement Blind Spot** | a disc-swelling sign — papilloedema, papillitis, drusen. It is not on `L11`'s list, though the bank treats it as a true glaucomatous change in Q448 and Q456. Real, but not *the* specific change, which is what this stem asks for. |\n| **C. Centrocaecal** | a scotoma joining fixation to the blind spot — the signature of **optic neuritis** and of toxic and nutritional optic neuropathies. It attacks central vision first, the opposite of glaucoma's pattern. |\n| **D. Altitudinal** | a defect respecting the horizontal midline across the *whole* half-field — classically **anterior ischaemic optic neuropathy**, where a disc infarct takes out one altitude of nerve head at a stroke. Q468 keys it as the change that is *not* glaucomatous. |\n\n⚠️ Note the tension inside this bank: option A here is a distractor, but Q448 and Q456 both list blind-spot enlargement among the true glaucomatous changes. Both keys are recorded as printed. The reconciliation is that the stem here asks for the **specific** change and the box answers with the arcuate scotoma; there is no key to dispute.",
    objective:'The specific POAG field defects are paracentral scotomata in Bjerrum’s area, the arcuate scotoma, the nasal step, and eventually a tubular field — arcuate because nerve fibres arch around the macula and stop at the horizontal raphe.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.72 (Q462), key p.77' },

  { id:'opqb-t11-463', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-glauc',
    stem:'In **phacomorphic glaucoma**:',
    options:['There is severe pain','It responds well to pilocarpine','It is usually associated with a deep anterior chamber','There is always iridodonesis'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**Phacomorphic glaucoma is an acute angle closure with a swollen lens behind it, so it hurts as much as any acute attack.**\n\n`L17) Cataract I` gives the mechanism in one line: \"Lens induced glaucoma → **Phacomorphic** → Intumescence (lens fibres fluid imbibition) → increased lens thickness → **pupil block** → elevated IOP\". `L18) Cataract II`'s summary table adds the clinical picture: \"Phacomorphic Glaucoma — Immature cataract — Swollen lens → angle closure — **Red eye + pain + HM** — Raised IOP, corneal oedema — Urgent phaco\".\n\nPupil block is `L12)`'s acute-attack mechanism, and `L12` gives the acute attack \"Pain → **++++**\". Same mechanism, same symptom. Option A is the key.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong.**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **B. It responds well to pilocarpine** | pilocarpine works by pulling the peripheral iris out of the angle, which helps when the block is purely pupillary and the lens is normal. Here the lens is **physically larger**, pushing the iris–lens diaphragm forward. Constricting the pupil tightens iris–lens contact and can make the block worse. `L18` puts \"**Urgent phaco**\" — removing the lens — in the mandatory-surgery column, and Q478 in this topic keys lens extraction for exactly this presentation. |\n| **C. Deep anterior chamber** | the reverse. A swollen lens shallows the chamber; that is the whole mechanism. `L18` describes it as an \"Immature cataract … swollen lens → angle closure\", and `L12` lists \"Shallow anterior chamber / Iris bombe (forward bowing)\" among the signs of the acute attack. Q478's stem names the shallow chamber outright. |\n| **D. Always iridodonesis** | iridodonesis — a wobbling iris on eye movement — means the lens is **missing or loose**, so the iris has lost its backing. It belongs to aphakia and to subluxation. `L18` files it with lens dislocation, not with intumescence. The phacomorphic lens is not loose; it is fat. \"Always\" makes it doubly wrong. |\n\n**Holding phacomorphic and phacolytic apart.** `L18` sets them side by side in its own exam-question format: \"Q: Phacomorphic vs phacolytic glaucoma? A: **Phacomorphic**: swollen immature lens → angle closure. **Phacolytic**: mature → protein leaks → macrophage blockage of TM.\" Its table repeats the split — phacomorphic arises from an *immature* cataract and closes the angle; phacolytic arises from a *hypermature or mature* one and clogs an open one. Q466, Q489 and Q495 in this topic are all the phacolytic side of that pair.\n\n*(Secondary chapter: `op-cat` — the mechanism is taught in the cataract decks, and the near-twin questions `opqb-t9-351` and `opqb-t9-405` are filed there.)*\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Phacomorphic glaucoma is acute angle closure caused by an intumescent lens — painful, with a shallow chamber, unresponsive to pilocarpine and cured by removing the lens; iridodonesis belongs to a loose or absent lens, not a swollen one.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.72 (Q463), key p.77' },

  { id:'opqb-t11-464', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-glauc',
    stem:'In **acute angle closure glaucoma**, the choice of surgery between **peripheral iridectomy or filtering operation** is decided by:',
    options:['Level of IOP','Indirect ophthalmoscopy','Gonioscopy','The condition of the other eye'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**The two operations treat two different states of the angle, so the angle has to be looked at — and looking at the angle is gonioscopy.**\n\n`L11) Glaucoma Diag, POAG` and `L12` both define the examination the same way: \"Gonioscopy → visualization of the angle of the anterior chamber\", distinguishing an angle whose \"outflow route (trabecular meshwork) **visibly exposed** to the aqueous humour\" from one where it is \"**not visibly exposed**\".\n\nThat distinction is exactly the surgical decision:\n\n| Angle on gonioscopy | What it means | Operation |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Appositionally closed, no adhesions** — the iris is lying against the meshwork but has not stuck to it | pupil block alone; the meshwork underneath still works | **Peripheral iridectomy / iridotomy** — make a hole, the iris falls back, the angle reopens. `L12`: \"Iridotomy — Laser (standard), Surgical\" |\n| **Permanently closed by peripheral anterior synechiae** | the iris has adhered to the meshwork; opening the pupil block will not peel it off, and the drainage route is gone for good | **Filtering surgery — trabeculectomy.** `L11` describes it as \"Creation of a fistula between the anterior chamber and the subconjunctival space\", i.e. a new drain that bypasses the angle altogether |\n\nAn iridectomy in an eye whose angle is sealed by synechiae relieves nothing; a trabeculectomy in an eye with a simple pupil block is a large operation for a problem a laser could have solved in minutes.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong.**\n\n| Option | Why it does not decide |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Level of IOP** | tells you how bad the obstruction is, not what kind it is. A pressure of 55 can come from either state; a pressure brought down medically has not changed the anatomy. |\n| **B. Indirect ophthalmoscopy** | examines the retina and the disc. It will show the cupping, but the cupping is the *consequence*, and it says nothing about whether the angle is stuck. `L12` also notes that in the acute attack \"Fundus examination, gonioscopy → usually **not possible**\" because of corneal oedema — one reason the angle is assessed after the pressure has been brought down. |\n| **D. The condition of the other eye** | matters enormously for the fellow eye's own prophylaxis — Q449 keys laser iridotomy for it — but it does not tell you what has happened in the eye you are about to operate on. |\n\n**The same principle, three times in this topic.** Q476 keys gonioscopy as the next step in a prodromal case, with its printed box saying it is \"important to rule out peripheral anterior synechiae\". Q493 keys \"Examine and decide according to the Angle State\" once the acute attack has subsided. Q464 is the same rule stated as a surgical choice. **The angle decides the operation.**\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Gonioscopy decides between iridectomy and filtering surgery in angle closure: an appositionally closed angle reopens once pupil block is relieved, whereas an angle sealed by peripheral anterior synechiae needs a fistula that bypasses it.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.72 (Q464), key p.77' },

  { id:'opqb-t11-465', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-glauc',
    stem:'In **secondary glaucoma due to acute iridocyclitis**, we better avoid the use of:',
    options:['Beta blockers.','Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors.','Pilocarpine.','Atropine.'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**In an inflamed eye the pupil must be kept wide, so the one antiglaucoma drug to avoid is the miotic.**\n\n`L13,14) Uvea & Sclera` gives the treatment of anterior uveitis and puts the mydriatic at the centre of it: \"Cycloplegic-mydriatic topical medications: **to decrease photophobia and pain from iritis and ciliary spasm**; **to prevent and break recently formed posterior synechiae**\", alongside \"**Antiglaucoma medications in case of increased IOP**\". Those two lines are the whole question — you treat the pressure, but not with anything that constricts the pupil.\n\n**Why pilocarpine is the wrong drug here.**\n\n- **It fights the pupil in the wrong direction.** Posterior synechiae form where the iris touches the anterior lens capsule in an eye full of fibrinous exudate. A constricted pupil maximises that contact around its whole circumference. If the pupil then seals completely, aqueous cannot reach the anterior chamber at all and the iris balloons forward — the same iris bombé `L12` draws for angle closure, now produced by inflammation. `L13,14` lists \"Posterior synechiae\" and \"Secondary glaucoma\" together as the sequelae of anterior uveitis.\n- **It worsens the pain.** `L13,14` prescribes cycloplegia specifically to relieve \"pain from iritis and **ciliary spasm**\". Pilocarpine is a cholinergic agonist: it contracts the ciliary muscle, which is exactly the spasm being treated.\n- **It increases inflammation and vascular leak.** Miotics increase blood–aqueous barrier permeability, adding to the protein already in the chamber. *(This last mechanism is **not taken from the course material**; the decks give the synechiae and spasm arguments only.)*\n\n**Why the other three are safe, and how they work.** `L11) Glaucoma Diag, POAG` puts all three on the aqueous-production line: \"Decreasing aqueous humour production → **beta-blockers, carbonic anhydrase inhibitors**, alpha agonists\". A drug that turns the tap down does not care what the iris is doing, so none of them disturbs the pupil or the inflammation. In uveitic glaucoma that is precisely why the production-lowering classes are preferred: `L11`'s own mechanism for uveitic secondary glaucoma is \"Deposition of inflammatory cells and plasmoid (viscid) aqueous humour → decreased permeability of the trabecular meshwork\" — the drain is blocked, so the only useful lever is the tap.\n\n**⚠️ Read this against Q450 and Q459, which look like the opposite.** Those two key **atropine** as the drug to avoid or the drug that precipitates glaucoma; this one keys atropine as *safe* and names pilocarpine instead. There is no contradiction, and no key to dispute:\n\n| Situation | Angle | Pupil you want | Drug forbidden |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Predisposed narrow angle (Q450, Q459) | crowded, closable | small or untouched | **atropine** — mydriasis starts pupil block |\n| Uveitic secondary glaucoma (Q465) | open, meshwork clogged by cells | **wide**, to break synechiae | **pilocarpine** — miosis welds the iris to the lens |\n\nThe drug is not good or bad in itself. **What the pupil must be doing depends on why the pressure is up.**\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'In glaucoma secondary to acute iridocyclitis the pupil must be kept dilated to break posterior synechiae and relieve ciliary spasm, so pilocarpine is avoided; pressure is controlled with aqueous-suppressants instead.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.72 (Q465), key p.77' },

  { id:'opqb-t11-466', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-glauc',
    stem:'**Phacolytic glaucoma** may occur in:',
    options:['Immature senile cortical cataract.','Nuclear cataract.','Intumescent cataract.','Hyper mature senile cataract.'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**Phacolytic glaucoma needs lens protein to leak out of the capsule, and only a hypermature cataract has liquefied cortex under a degenerate capsule.**\n\n`L17) Cataract I` gives the mechanism in full: \"Lens induced glaucoma → **Phacolytic** → **Degeneration of lens capsule → leakage of lens proteins** → immune reaction in anterior chamber (uveitis) → uveitic glaucoma; **clogging of TM by lens proteins macromolecules → secondary open angle glaucoma (trabecular)**\". `L18) Cataract II`'s summary table names the stage: \"Phacolytic Glaucoma — **Hypermature/mature** — Liquefied cortex leaks proteins — HM, pain, redness — **Macrophages block TM** — Urgent lens removal\". Its own exam-format slide puts it in a sentence: \"Phacolytic: **mature → protein leaks → macrophage blockage of TM**\".\n\n`L18` also describes what a hypermature lens looks like: \"**SHRUNKEN type:** cortex leaks → wrinkled capsule. **MORGAGNIAN type:** liquefied cortex, nucleus sinks inferiorly (pathognomonic pseudohypopyon-like appearance). **Risk: phacolytic glaucoma**\" — the deck attaches the risk to that stage by name.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong.**\n\n| Option | Stage | Why not |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A. Immature senile cortical cataract** | early | the capsule is intact and the cortex is still solid. Nothing to leak. This is the stage that turns **intumescent** and gives *phacomorphic* glaucoma instead. |\n| **B. Nuclear cataract** | sclerosis of the nucleus | a hardening, not a liquefaction. The cortex stays clear and the capsule stays competent; a nuclear cataract can sit for years without any glaucoma. |\n| **C. Intumescent cataract** | swollen | ⚠️ the trap. `L17` gives intumescence its own line — \"Intumescence (lens fibres liquefaction and fluid imbibition → **swelling of lens**)\" — and its glaucoma is the *other* one: \"Phacomorphic → increased lens thickness → **pupil block** → elevated IOP\". A swollen lens shuts the angle mechanically; it does not leak protein through the meshwork. |\n\n**The two lens-induced glaucomas, held apart.** `L18` prints the comparison as a question and answer, and it is worth carrying whole: **phacomorphic** — immature, swollen lens, angle **closure**, mechanical; **phacolytic** — mature or hypermature, leaked protein and macrophages, angle **open**, obstructive. Both are cured by removing the lens. Q463 in this topic is the phacomorphic side; Q489 asks the treatment of the phacolytic side; Q495 uses both as distractors.\n\n**⚠️ Note for the sweep.** `opqb-t9-405` already in the corpus asks \"Phacolytic glaucoma occurs in:\" and keys \"Hyper mature cortical cataract\" — the same stem and the same key, but with a **different option menu** (congenital / early cortical / subluxation / hypermature cortical against this one's immature senile cortical / nuclear / intumescent / hypermature senile). Flagged as a stem match with replaced distractors, not a clean reprint.\n\n*(Secondary chapter: `op-cat` — `opqb-t9-405` and `opqb-t9-351` are filed there.)*\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Phacolytic glaucoma arises in a hypermature cataract, where a degenerate capsule lets liquefied cortical protein escape and macrophages clog an open trabecular meshwork — the intumescent lens causes the phacomorphic, angle-closure variety instead.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.72 (Q466), key p.77' },

  { id:'opqb-t11-467', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-glauc',
    stem:'During **gonioscopy**, the following structures can be seen **EXCEPT**:',
    options:['Iris root.','Ciliary body.','Canal of Schlemm.',"Schwalbe's line"],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**Schlemm's canal is inside the sclera, behind the trabecular meshwork — a gonioscopist sees the meshwork, never the canal.**\n\n`L1,2) Ocular Anatomy, Symptomatology & Basic Eye Examination` teaches the gonioscopic view with a mnemonic on slide 22, read outwards from the iris: **\"I CAN'T SEE THE STUPID LINE\"** —\n\n| Mnemonic word | Structure |\n|---|---|\n| **I** | Iris |\n| **CAN'T** | Ciliary body |\n| **SEE** | Scleral spur |\n| **THE** | Trabecular Meshwork |\n| **STUPID LINE** | Schwalbe's Line |\n\nThat is the complete list of what is visible, and the canal of Schlemm is not on it. Slides 23 and 24 repeat the same labels on real gonioscopic photographs — one captioned \"Open Angle ⇢ Narrow Angle ⇢ Closed Angle\", one showing peripheral anterior synechiae with the abbreviations SL, TM, SS, CB and Iris. **Five structures, five labels, no canal.**\n\n**Why the canal cannot be seen.** `L11) Glaucoma Diag, POAG` traces the outflow route: \"Aqueous humour drainage → **trabecular meshwork → Schlemm's canal → collector channels** → systemic circulation\". The meshwork is the innermost element and faces the anterior chamber; the canal lies *behind* it, embedded in the corneoscleral tissue at the limbus. Gonioscopy is a surface view of the recess, so it reaches as far as the meshwork's inner face and stops there. The canal is seen histologically, or by imaging, or by a surgeon who has cut down onto it — not through a goniolens.\n\n**Why the other three are seen.**\n\n| Option | Where it sits in the view |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Iris root** | the innermost landmark, where the iris turns to insert into the ciliary body. `L1,2`'s \"I\". |\n| **B. Ciliary body** | the ciliary band, a grey-brown strip immediately beyond the iris root. `L1,2`'s \"CAN'T\", abbreviated CB on slide 24. |\n| **D. Schwalbe's line** | the outermost landmark, the termination of Descemet's membrane. `L1,2`'s \"STUPID LINE\", abbreviated SL. |\n\n**Why it matters clinically.** The whole classification turns on how many of these five you can see. `L11`'s definition — \"Open angle → outflow route (trabecular meshwork) **visibly exposed** to the aqueous humour; Closed angle → … **not visibly exposed**\" — is graded in practice by counting landmarks from Schwalbe's line inwards. See the ciliary band and the angle is wide open; see only Schwalbe's line and it is nearly shut. `L1,2` slide 24 shows what a permanently damaged angle looks like: peripheral anterior synechiae bridging the recess, which is what Q464, Q476 and Q493 are all trying to find.\n\n*(Secondary chapter: `op-intro` — the mnemonic and the angle anatomy are taught in the anatomy deck, not in either glaucoma deck.)*\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Gonioscopy shows five landmarks — iris root, ciliary band, scleral spur, trabecular meshwork and Schwalbe’s line; Schlemm’s canal lies behind the meshwork within the sclera and is never visible.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.72 (Q467), key p.77' },

  { id:'opqb-t11-468', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-glauc',
    stem:'All of the following are **characteristic field changes in primary open angle glaucoma EXCEPT**:',
    options:['Altitudinal field defect.','Arcuate scotoma.','Nasal step.','Any scotoma in Bjerrum area'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**Three of these four are on `L11`'s list of POAG field changes; the altitudinal defect is not — it belongs to an infarcted disc.**\n\n`L11) Glaucoma Diag, POAG` prints the list twice. Slide 15: \"**Paracentral scotomata in Bjerrum's area (10 – 20 degrees of central field)** · **Arcuate scotomata (superior, inferior)** · **Nasal step** · Ring scotoma (double arcuate scotoma) · Constriction of visual field (→ tubular field) · Temporal island of vision\". The summary diagram on slides 3 and 22 repeats \"Bjerrum scotoma · Arcuate scotoma · Nasal step · Ring scotoma · Tubular field / temporal island\". Options B, C and D are the first three names on both lists.\n\n**Why an altitudinal defect is a different disease.** An altitudinal defect takes out the *whole* upper or lower half-field at once, respecting the horizontal midline across its entire width. That is what happens when the blood supply to one half of the optic nerve head fails in a single event — anterior ischaemic optic neuropathy. Glaucoma does not work that way: it kills nerve fibre bundles gradually and asymmetrically, so its defects are arcs and steps rather than clean halves. `L11`'s structural counterpart says the same thing — the rim thins \"**Focal → polar notch → superior, inferior**\" or \"Circumferential → generalized thinning\". A notch produces an arc; it does not produce an altitude.\n\n**The point of confusion, and why it is not one.** The nasal step *also* respects the horizontal midline — the box on Q462 explains it as \"asymmetric involvement of the arcuate nerve bundles above and below the fixation point\". So both defects have a horizontal edge. The difference is extent: a **step** is a small offset where two arcuate defects of unequal size meet nasally; an **altitude** is the entire half-field gone. Glaucoma produces the step; ischaemia produces the altitude.\n\n| Option | Disease it points to |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Altitudinal field defect** | **anterior ischaemic optic neuropathy — the key**; also seen with a branch retinal artery or vein occlusion respecting the horizontal |\n| **B. Arcuate scotoma** | POAG; `L11` slide 15 |\n| **C. Nasal step** | POAG; `L11` slide 15 and the summary diagram |\n| **D. Any scotoma in Bjerrum area** | POAG; `L11` names Bjerrum's area explicitly and Q462's printed box calls the paracentral zone \"the first to be affected in POAG\" |\n\n⚠️ **`Bjerrum` was checked, and the hit was opened before anything was written about it.** It occurs in exactly two cached decks: `L11`, where it names the 10–20° zone, and `L1,2) Ocular Anatomy…`, where it appears only inside that deck's own coverage note recording that its perimetry slide is bare — \"**'Bjerrum' = 0**\" for the examination deck. So the term is taught, and it is taught in the glaucoma deck, which is where this question needs it.\n\n**Shared table:** the full field list lives in `opqb-t11-462`, which carries the bank's printed box.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Arcuate scotomata, the nasal step and paracentral scotomata in Bjerrum’s area are the characteristic POAG field defects; an altitudinal defect losing a whole half-field points to anterior ischaemic optic neuropathy instead.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.72 (Q468), key p.77' },

  { id:'opqb-t11-469', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-glauc',
    stem:'All of these drugs **increase aqueous drainage EXCEPT**:',
    options:['Prostaglandins.','Alpha adrenergic blockers.','Cholinergic agonists.','Beta blockers.'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**Beta-blockers** **reduce aqueous production,** do not increase outflow; prostaglandins, cholinergics, and alpha agonists increase drainage.\n\n---\n\n*(The lines above are the bank's own printed explanation, transcribed verbatim. The notes below are added for study.)*\n\n**This is Q457 asked from the other end**, and `L11) Glaucoma Diag, POAG` answers both from the same slide: \"Medical treatment → Decreasing aqueous humour production → **beta-blockers**, carbonic anhydrase inhibitors, alpha agonists. Increasing aqueous humour drainage → **prostaglandin analogues, miotics, alpha agonists**.\"\n\nBeta-blockers appear on the production line and nowhere else. They are the exception. The shared two-column table is written out in full in `opqb-t11-457`.\n\n**⚠️ A source misprint in option B, repaired here and quoted.** The bank prints \"**Alpha adrenergic blockers**\". `L11` names \"alpha **agonists**\" — twice, once on each line — and the bank's own printed box for this very question says \"prostaglandins, cholinergics, and **alpha agonists** increase drainage\". The box contradicts the option it is explaining. The class used in glaucoma is the alpha-2 **agonist** (brimonidine, apraclonidine); an alpha *blocker* is an antihypertensive with no established ocular hypotensive role. Read option B as *alpha adrenergic agonists*; the printed word is **\"blockers\"**, and it is a misprint, not a distractor. The key does not move — beta-blockers are still the exception on either reading, because alpha agonists are on both of `L11`'s lines while beta-blockers are on only one.\n\n**Why each of the other three does increase drainage.**\n\n| Option | Route |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Prostaglandins** | `L11` puts \"prostaglandin analogues\" on the drainage line. They open the **uveoscleral** pathway — outflow through the ciliary muscle bundles rather than through the meshwork. *(The uveoscleral route is named in the bank's Q457 box but not in `L11`, which lists only the trabecular route; it is **not taken from the course material**.)* |\n| **B. Alpha agonists** *(as repaired)* | `L11` lists them on **both** lines. They reduce production and increase uveoscleral outflow, which is why they can answer either half of this pair. |\n| **C. Cholinergic agonists** | `L11`'s \"miotics\". Pilocarpine contracts the ciliary muscle, which pulls on the scleral spur and mechanically opens the trabecular meshwork — and constricts the pupil, pulling the peripheral iris out of a narrow angle. Both effects increase trabecular drainage. |\n| **D. Beta blockers** | **the key.** Production only: they suppress cyclic-AMP-driven secretion by the ciliary epithelium. Nothing they do touches either outflow route. |\n\n**Where the two routes actually are.** `L11`: \"Aqueous humour production → **ciliary processes around lens equator**\" … \"Aqueous humour drainage → **trabecular meshwork → Schlemm's canal → collector channels** → systemic circulation\". Every antiglaucoma drug acts at one end of that sentence or the other. Q484 asks which of them lowers pressure hardest.",
    objective:'Prostaglandin analogues, miotics and alpha agonists lower pressure by increasing outflow; beta-blockers act only by suppressing aqueous production at the ciliary epithelium.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.73 (Q469), key p.77' },

  { id:'opqb-t11-470', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-glauc',
    stem:'All of those findings are present in the **acute stage of primary angle closure glaucoma EXCEPT**:',
    options:['Increased IOP.','Dilated vertically oval pupil.','Constricted visual field.','Difficult fundus examination due to corneal edema.'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**A constricted visual field is a chronic finding — it takes months of nerve fibre loss. The acute attack is hours old.**\n\n`L12) Glaucoma 2 Cong, PACG, 2ry` lists the signs of acute angle closure and three of these four are on it verbatim:\n\n> \"Diminution of vision · **IOP → ++++ (stony hard)** · **Corneal oedema** — (Fundus examination, gonioscopy → usually not possible) · Ciliary injection · **Mid-dilated vertically oval non-reactive pupil** · Shallow anterior chamber / Iris bombe (forward bowing) · Eyelid oedema\"\n\nOption A is the deck's \"IOP → ++++\", option B its \"Mid-dilated vertically oval non-reactive pupil\", and option D is the deck's own parenthesis about the fundus. Only the constricted field is missing — and it is missing because it cannot be there yet.\n\n**Why a field defect takes time and a pressure spike does not.** `L12` describes the attack as a \"**Rapid severe rise of IOP**\" developing over hours: \"Diminution of vision → rapid (over several hours)\". The vision drops because the cornea has become oedematous and the media are cloudy, not because the nerve has been destroyed. Field constriction, by contrast, is the end point of `L11)`'s slow sequence — neural rim thinning → paracentral scotomata → arcuate scotomata → nasal step → ring scotoma → \"Constriction of visual field (→ tubular field)\". `L12` assigns exactly that picture to the **chronic** form instead: chronic angle closure glaucoma has a \"Clinical picture … similar to primary open angle glaucoma (elevation of IOP, progressive optic nerve cupping and **visual field changes**)\". Q472 is built on that same slide.\n\nThere is a practical corollary: **you could not measure the field in an acute attack even if a defect existed**, because the patient is in agony, vomiting, and looking through an oedematous cornea.\n\n**Why option B is the subtlest of the three true findings.** The pupil in an acute attack is *mid-dilated*, not widely dilated and not constricted, and it is *vertically oval* — the classic shape produced when the sphincter is ischaemic from the pressure and the iris stroma is pushed forward unevenly. The bank prints it as \"Dilated vertically oval pupil\" without `L12`'s \"mid-\", which is a slight loosening of the deck's wording; the finding is the same one, and it is genuinely present. Q481 in this topic asks about the pupil directly and keys \"Mid-dilated, non-reactive\", matching `L12` exactly.\n\n**Why option D matters clinically.** `L12`'s own parenthesis — \"Fundus examination, **gonioscopy → usually not possible**\" — is the reason Q464 and Q493 both defer the angle assessment until after the pressure has been brought down medically. You cannot see through an oedematous cornea, so the operation is chosen later.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'The acute angle closure attack shows a stony-hard eye, corneal oedema obscuring the fundus, ciliary injection and a mid-dilated vertically oval fixed pupil; a constricted visual field belongs to the chronic form, which takes months of nerve fibre loss.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.73 (Q470), key p.77' },

  { id:'opqb-t11-471', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-glauc',
    stem:'Regarding **neovascular glaucoma**:',
    options:['Can be caused by carotid artery insufficiency.','It is classified as secondary open angle glaucoma.','It can be easily controlled medically.','Peripheral iridectomy is indicated in the fellow eye'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**Anything that leaves the retina chronically hypoxic can drive rubeosis, and a stenosed carotid does exactly that.**\n\n`L12) Glaucoma 2 Cong, PACG, 2ry` gives neovascular glaucoma its mechanism — \"Neovascular membrane grows over the trabecular meshwork and **contracts pulling the peripheral iris over the trabeculum**\" — and lists causes that all reduce to one thing: an ischaemic posterior segment. Carotid insufficiency produces the same end state by starving the whole eye of arterial supply, chronically rather than suddenly.\n\n**⚠️ This is a gap in the material and it is filled, not declared.** I checked `carotid` across every cached source: it appears in `L1,2`, `L10) orbit`, `L19.2`, `L22`, `L31,32`, and in book chapters ch01, ch03 and ch14 — but in none of those is it linked to rubeosis or to neovascular glaucoma, and **neither glaucoma deck mentions the carotid at all**. The entity the option is pointing at is the ocular ischaemic syndrome, and that name appears in no cached deck or book chapter either. So: that carotid occlusive disease causes chronic ocular hypoperfusion, rubeosis iridis and neovascular glaucoma is **not taken from the course material**. The general principle underlying it — retinal ischaemia drives iris new vessels — is taught, in `L19.2)`'s \"Rubeosis irides in 50 %\" for ischaemic CRVO and in `L12`'s own list.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong, and option B is the one worth arguing.**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **B. Classified as secondary OPEN angle glaucoma** | ⚠️ **`L12` files it under \"Secondary NARROW angle glaucoma\"**, with \"anterior pulling\" as the mechanism, alongside iridocorneal endothelial syndrome, intraocular tumours and retinal vein occlusion. On the deck's own classification, B is false — which is what makes A the key. *(Clinically NVG does pass through an early open-angle stage, when new vessels lie across a still-open angle before the membrane contracts. That two-stage course is **not taken from the course material**; `L12` gives only the closed end state. Either way, the deck's classification is narrow angle, so B is not the answer.)* |\n| **C. Easily controlled medically** | the opposite. `L19.2) Retinal Vascular Disorders` calls ischaemic CRVO's prognosis \"**Very poor**\" and its treatment panretinal photocoagulation — an attack on the *cause*, because the pressure itself is not tractable. Once the angle is welded shut by a fibrovascular membrane, aqueous suppressants have nowhere to send the fluid. |\n| **D. Peripheral iridectomy in the fellow eye** | confuses two diseases. Prophylactic iridotomy belongs to **primary** angle closure, where both eyes share a crowded anterior segment and a pupil-block mechanism — that is Q449. In NVG the fellow eye is at risk only if it shares the *systemic* disease (diabetes, carotid stenosis) or has its own vein occlusion. There is no pupil block to pre-empt, so a hole in the iris achieves nothing. |\n\n**Why NVG is so hard to treat.** `L12`'s mechanism is mechanical and irreversible: a membrane grows, then contracts, and the peripheral iris is pulled permanently over the trabeculum — peripheral anterior synechiae, the same finding `L1,2` photographs on slide 24. Nothing reopens an angle that has been sutured shut by scar. This is why the treatment of the *retina* — panretinal photocoagulation before rubeosis appears — is the only reliable intervention, and why `L19.2` insists on monthly review for six months.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Neovascular glaucoma follows chronic retinal ischaemia from any cause, including carotid insufficiency; the deck classifies it as secondary narrow angle glaucoma, it responds poorly to medical treatment, and the fellow eye needs no iridectomy.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.73 (Q471), key p.77' },

  { id:'opqb-t11-472', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-glauc',
    stem:'Signs of **chronic angle closure glaucoma** can include all of the following **EXCEPT**:',
    options:['Shallow anterior chamber.','Constricted visual field.','Hyperemia of the optic disc.','Peripheral anterior synechia.'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**A glaucomatous disc is pale and excavated, not hyperaemic. Hyperaemia belongs to a swollen disc, which is the opposite appearance.**\n\n`L12) Glaucoma 2 Cong, PACG, 2ry` describes the chronic form in three columns: \"**Mechanism** — Repeated episodes of intermittent / acute angle closure attacks → **Chronic closure of the angle**. **Clinical picture** — Similar to primary open angle glaucoma (elevation of IOP, **progressive optic nerve cupping and visual field changes**). **Treatment** — Same protocol as open angle glaucoma.\"\n\nSo the chronic disc is `L11)`'s POAG disc: \"Neural rim thinning · Focal → polar notch → superior, inferior · Circumferential → generalized thinning of the neural rim · Clinically → enlargement of cup/disc ratio > 0.3\", with \"**Total cupping with nasal shift of blood vessels**\" in advanced disease. Rim tissue is being lost. A disc losing tissue is pale; a disc that is hyperaemic is one whose capillaries are engorged and whose margins are blurred — papilloedema, papillitis, anterior ischaemic optic neuropathy in its acute phase. **The one appearance excludes the other.**\n\n**Why the other three are true.**\n\n| Option | Where it comes from |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Shallow anterior chamber** | the anatomical substrate of the whole disease. `L12` lists \"Shallow anterior chamber / Iris bombe\" among the acute signs and puts \"crowded anterior segments\" at the head of its risk profile. The chamber does not deepen between attacks — it is why the attacks happen. |\n| **B. Constricted visual field** | `L12` gives the chronic form \"visual field changes\" explicitly, and `L11`'s list ends in \"Constriction of visual field (→ tubular field)\". ⚠️ Contrast Q470, where the same option is the **exception** — because that stem specifies the *acute* stage, in which there has been no time for field loss. The two keys are consistent; only the time course differs. |\n| **D. Peripheral anterior synechia** | the defining lesion of chronic closure. `L12`'s mechanism is \"repeated episodes … → chronic closure of the angle\", which is what PAS are — the iris adherent to the meshwork where it has lain against it too often. `L1,2) Ocular Anatomy…` slide 24 photographs them gonioscopically. This is exactly what Q476's printed box says gonioscopy is done to rule out, and what Q464 says decides the operation. |\n\n**The clinical shape of the disease.** `L12` draws chronic angle closure as one station on a continuum: \"Intermittent angle closure → Acute angle closure (crisis) → **Chronic narrow angle glaucoma** → Absolute glaucoma\", the last labelled \"**Blind painful eye**\". The chronic stage is the quiet one, and that is its danger — it looks and behaves like POAG, with no pain and no redness, while the angle silently seals.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Chronic angle closure glaucoma shows a shallow chamber, peripheral anterior synechiae, progressive cupping and field constriction — a picture like POAG; a hyperaemic disc indicates swelling, the opposite of glaucomatous excavation.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.73 (Q472), key p.77' },

  { id:'opqb-t11-473', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-uvea',
    stem:'A **45-year-old female** patient presented with **pain in her left eye & photophobia**. On examination there was **ciliary injection**, **cells in the anterior chamber**, a **miotic pupil** & **IOP of 27 mmhg**, the most likely diagnosis is:',
    options:['Acute keratitis.','Acute anterior uveitis.','Acute attack of angle closure glaucoma.','Acute viral conjunctivitis.'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**Cells in the anterior chamber with a small pupil is anterior uveitis, and the raised pressure is a complication of it rather than a competing diagnosis.**\n\n`L13,14) Uvea & Sclera` gives anterior uveitis its signs — \"Keratic precipitates (KPs) on the back surface of cornea\", \"**Cells & flare** ± hypopyon\" — and its sequelae: \"Posterior synechiae · **Secondary glaucoma**\", with the deck showing photographs of pupils \"grossly distorted into festooned/irregular shapes by posterior synechiae\". Its treatment slide adds \"**Antiglaucoma medications in case of increased IOP**\", which tells you the combination in this stem is expected, not contradictory.\n\n**Reading the four findings.**\n\n| Finding | What it narrows |\n|---|---|\n| Pain and photophobia | any anterior segment inflammation; does not discriminate |\n| **Ciliary injection** | inflammation deep to the conjunctiva — circumcorneal, from the anterior ciliary vessels. Excludes conjunctivitis, which injects the fornices |\n| **Cells in the anterior chamber** | inflammatory cells free in the aqueous. The single most specific finding here |\n| **Miotic pupil** | ciliary spasm and iris sphincter irritation |\n| **IOP 27 mmHg** | mildly raised — the trabecular meshwork clogged by inflammatory cells |\n\n**Why the other three are wrong.**\n\n| Option | The discriminator |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Acute keratitis** | the stem describes no corneal lesion — no ulcer, no infiltrate, no fluorescein staining, no epithelial defect. `L15,16) Cornea` builds its keratitis picture on the corneal surface, which is silent here. |\n| **C. Acute attack of angle closure glaucoma** | ⚠️ **the pupil and the pressure both refute it.** `L12) Glaucoma 2 Cong, PACG, 2ry` gives the acute attack a \"**Mid-dilated vertically oval non-reactive pupil**\" — this pupil is *miotic*. And it gives it \"IOP → **++++ (stony hard)**\" with \"corneal oedema\" and \"Diminution of vision → rapid\" — 27 mmHg is raised but nowhere near that, and no visual loss is described. A miotic pupil at 27 mmHg is an inflamed eye, not an obstructed one. |\n| **D. Acute viral conjunctivitis** | conjunctival, superficial and usually bilateral with a follicular reaction and watery discharge. It does not put cells in the anterior chamber, does not cause ciliary injection, and does not raise the pressure. |\n\n**Why the raised pressure is the trap.** The question sits inside a *glaucoma* chapter, and it prints an IOP. Both facts pull toward option C. `L11) Glaucoma Diag, POAG` is what defuses it: the deck lists \"**Uveitic**\" among the causes of secondary *open* angle glaucoma — \"Deposition of inflammatory cells and plasmoid (viscid) aqueous humour → decreased permeability of the trabecular meshwork to aqueous humour\". The pressure in this eye is up **because** of the uveitis. Q465 in this topic asks how to treat exactly this pressure, and keys avoiding pilocarpine.\n\n**Q474 continues this vignette.**\n\n*(Secondary chapter: `op-glauc` — the raised pressure is secondary glaucoma from uveitis, which is why the bank prints the question here.)*\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Ciliary injection with cells in the anterior chamber, a miotic pupil and a modestly raised pressure is acute anterior uveitis with secondary glaucoma — an acute angle closure attack would give a stony-hard eye and a mid-dilated fixed pupil.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.73 (Q473), key p.77' },

  { id:'opqb-t11-474', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-uvea',
    stem:'In the **previous condition** — the 45-year-old woman with a painful, photophobic left eye showing **ciliary injection, cells in the anterior chamber, a miotic pupil and an IOP of 27 mmHg** — the **main line of treatment** is:',
    options:['Prostaglandin eye drops.','Antibiotic drops.','Antiviral drops.','Corticosteroid eye drops.'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**The findings in the stem are inflammatory, and the main line of treatment for intraocular inflammation is a topical steroid.**\n\n`L13,14) Uvea & Sclera` sets out the treatment of anterior uveitis, and the steroid heads it. Alongside it the deck lists \"Cycloplegic-mydriatic topical medications: to decrease photophobia and pain from iritis and ciliary spasm; to prevent and break recently formed posterior synechiae\", \"**Antiglaucoma medications in case of increased IOP**\", \"Control of the causative condition (after doing a systemic work up especially in recurrent cases…)\" and \"Treatment of secondary ocular complications\".\n\nSo the full regimen has four parts — steroid, cycloplegic, pressure control, and a hunt for the systemic cause — and the question asks for the *main line*. That is the anti-inflammatory. Everything else either supports it or cleans up after it.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong.**\n\n| Option | What it treats | Why not here |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A. Prostaglandin eye drops** | ⚠️ an antiglaucoma agent — `L11) Glaucoma Diag, POAG` puts \"prostaglandin analogues\" on the drainage-increasing line. Tempting, because the pressure is 27. But it treats a *number*, not the disease making the number, and prostaglandin analogues are pro-inflammatory agents that are generally avoided in an actively inflamed eye. *(That prostaglandin analogues are relatively contraindicated in uveitis is **not taken from the course material**; `L11` lists the class without cautions.)* If pressure control is needed, `L11`'s production-suppressing line is the safer half — and Q465 keys the drug to avoid there as pilocarpine. |\n| **B. Antibiotic drops** | bacterial surface infection | there is no discharge, no ulcer and no conjunctival picture in the stem. Antibiotics do not reach the anterior chamber in useful concentration topically and there is nothing there to kill. |\n| **C. Antiviral drops** | herpetic epithelial keratitis | the stem describes no corneal lesion at all. A dendritic ulcer would have been named. |\n\n**Why the steroid is safe here despite what it does to pressure.** `L11` lists \"**Steroid induced**\" among the causes of secondary open angle glaucoma — \"Prolonged intake of potent steroids in a predisposed individual → decreased permeability of the trabecular meshwork\". So the drug that treats this eye is also a drug that can raise pressure. The resolution is in `L11`'s own wording: the risk is from **prolonged** use in a **predisposed** individual. Here the pressure is already raised by the inflammation itself, and controlling the inflammation is what will bring it down; the steroid is then tapered and the pressure watched. That tension — treat with the drug that could cause the problem, then withdraw it — is why uveitic eyes are followed closely rather than discharged on drops.\n\n**Restated antecedent.** The original stem (Q473) reads: \"A 45-year-old female patient presented with pain in her left eye & photophobia. On examination there was ciliary injection, cells in the anterior chamber, a miotic pupil & IOP of 27 mmhg\". The findings are restated above; the diagnosis is deliberately not, since that is Q473's own answer.\n\n*(Secondary chapter: `op-glauc` — the pressure in this eye is secondary glaucoma, and Q465 covers how to lower it.)*\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Acute anterior uveitis is treated principally with topical corticosteroid, supported by a cycloplegic-mydriatic, pressure control if needed, and a systemic work-up for the cause.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.73 (Q474), key p.77' },

  { id:'opqb-t11-475', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-glauc',
    stem:'A **55-year-old female** patient came complaining of **headache, blurring of vision & redness in the right eye since one week**. On examination, her **BCVA was 6/6 in right eye with +4 sphere & 6/6 in left eye with +4 sphere**, right eye was **quiet**, anterior chamber was **shallow**, pupil was **briskly reactive** & **IOP was 12 mmhg**. The most probable diagnosis is:',
    options:['Attack of acute iridocyclitis','Mucopurulent conjunctivitis','Prodromal attack of narrow angle glaucoma','Chronic stage of narrow angle glaucoma'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"The clue to the diagnosis is she is a **hyperopic patient** with a shallow AC, but with normal IOP & normal vision (so the attack is resolved)\n\n---\n\n*(The lines above are the bank's own printed explanation, transcribed verbatim. The notes below are added for study.)*\n\n**The +4 sphere is the whole question.** `L7,8) Refractive errors` gives hyperopia's complications and the first is this: \"**Predisposition to narrow angle glaucoma due to shallow anterior chamber** (especially later in life as the lens becomes thicker)\". `L12) Glaucoma 2 Cong, PACG, 2ry` says the same thing from the other side, listing among those at risk for angle closure — \"crowded anterior segments\" — \"Females · **Hyperopes** · Race (South-East Asians) · Advancing age · Affected fellow eye\". A 55-year-old woman with +4 in both eyes is three of those five.\n\n**Why the examination is normal, and why that is the point.** `L12` draws angle closure as a continuum: \"**Intermittent angle closure** → Acute angle closure (crisis) → Chronic narrow angle glaucoma → Absolute glaucoma\", and gives the first station its own slide:\n\n> \"**Intermittent (subacute) Angle Closure Attacks** — Short episodes of angle closure · **Attacks resolve spontaneously** · Mild symptoms: minimal or no blurring of vision, **coloured haloes around light**, eye pain, brow ache or **headache** · Attacks resolve by **bright illumination** and **sleep** · Treatment: **Iridotomy — Laser, Surgical**\"\n\nEvery normal finding in the stem is therefore diagnostic rather than reassuring. The eye is quiet, the pupil reacts briskly and the pressure is 12 **because the episode is over** — that is what \"resolve spontaneously\" means. What is left behind is the anatomy: the shallow chamber, which does not resolve.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong.**\n\n| Option | The discriminator |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Attack of acute iridocyclitis** | an inflamed eye is not \"quiet\". `L13,14) Uvea & Sclera` requires cells and flare, keratic precipitates, ciliary injection; the pupil would be miotic or festooned by synechiae, not briskly reactive. Q473 shows what that eye looks like. |\n| **B. Mucopurulent conjunctivitis** | discharge, a red fornix, no headache, and nothing whatever to do with the anterior chamber depth. The stem gives no discharge. |\n| **D. Chronic stage of narrow angle glaucoma** | ⚠️ the real competitor, and the pressure kills it. `L12` gives the chronic stage \"elevation of IOP, progressive optic nerve cupping and visual field changes\". This patient's IOP is **12** and her acuity is **6/6 in both eyes**. Nothing chronic has happened yet. |\n\n**Why this diagnosis is worth making.** The prodromal stage is the only point at which the disease can be stopped at no cost. `L12` prints one treatment under the intermittent-attack slide — **iridotomy** — and the same operation, offered a month later after a crisis, has to be done in an inflamed eye with a cloudy cornea and possibly a partly synechiated angle. Q476 asks what to do next, and the answer is to look at the angle before deciding.\n\n**A note on the halos.** Q483 in this topic describes exactly the same symptom in a patient who did *not* get away with it — \"halos of light after being in the dark, suddenly developed severe headache, drop of vision and red painful eye\". The prodrome and the crisis are the same mechanism, hours apart.",
    objective:'Headache and blurring in a hyperopic middle-aged woman whose eye is now quiet with a shallow chamber and normal pressure is a resolved intermittent (prodromal) angle-closure attack — the trigger for prophylactic iridotomy before a crisis.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.73 (Q475), key p.78' },

  { id:'opqb-t11-476', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-glauc',
    stem:'The most appropriate step in the management of the **previous case** — the 55-year-old **+4 hyperope** with a week of headache and blurring whose right eye is now **quiet**, with a **shallow anterior chamber**, a **briskly reactive pupil** and an **IOP of 12 mmHg** — is:',
    options:['Perform gonioscopy examination','Administers steroid eye drops','Administers antibiotic eye drops','Do trabeculectomy operation'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**Gonioscopy** is important to rule out peripheral anterior synechiae\n\n---\n\n*(The line above is the bank's own printed explanation, transcribed verbatim. The notes below are added for study.)*\n\n**The eye is quiet, so this is the moment to look at the angle.** `L11) Glaucoma Diag, POAG` and `L12` both define gonioscopy as \"visualization of the angle of the anterior chamber\", and make it the examination that separates \"Open angle → outflow route (trabecular meshwork) **visibly exposed** to the aqueous humour\" from \"Closed angle → … **not visibly exposed**\". In an eye with a shallow chamber and a history of episodes, that is the only outstanding question: is the angle merely narrow, or has it begun to stick?\n\n**Why peripheral anterior synechiae are what you are hunting for.** `L12`'s continuum runs \"Intermittent angle closure → Acute angle closure (crisis) → **Chronic narrow angle glaucoma** → Absolute glaucoma\", and the chronic stage arises from \"**Repeated episodes** of intermittent / acute angle closure attacks → **Chronic closure of the angle**\". PAS are how that closure becomes permanent: iris tissue that has lain against the meshwork often enough adheres to it. `L1,2) Ocular Anatomy, Symptomatology & Basic Eye Examination` photographs them on slide 24, with an arrow to \"Peripheral anterior synechiae … bridging the angle\" and the landmarks SL, TM, SS, CB labelled around it.\n\nThe finding changes the plan. An angle that is narrow but free reopens once the pupil block is relieved — **laser iridotomy**, which `L12` prints as the treatment under its own intermittent-attack slide and calls the \"standard\". An angle already sealed by synechiae will not reopen, and the eye needs filtering surgery instead. Q464 states this rule as a surgical choice, and Q493 states it as a next step after a crisis. **The angle decides.**\n\n**Why the other three are wrong.**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **B. Steroid eye drops** | there is nothing to suppress. The eye is described as quiet, with a briskly reactive pupil and no cells — the opposite of the inflamed eye in Q473. ⚠️ Worse, `L11` lists \"**Steroid induced**\" secondary open angle glaucoma, so steroids in an eye already at risk of raised pressure add a second mechanism for no benefit. |\n| **C. Antibiotic eye drops** | no discharge, no infiltrate, no infection. This option exists to catch anyone who read \"redness\" and stopped. |\n| **D. Trabeculectomy** | `L11` describes it as \"Creation of a fistula between the anterior chamber and the subconjunctival space\" — a filtering operation for an eye whose pressure cannot be controlled. This patient's IOP is **12**, her acuity is 6/6, and her angle has not been examined. Operating before looking is exactly the error the key is testing. |\n\n**The sequence, stated once.** Symptoms suggest the mechanism → gonioscopy establishes the state of the angle → the state of the angle selects the operation. Skipping the middle step is how an eye gets an iridotomy that cannot work, or a trabeculectomy it never needed.\n\n**Restated antecedent.** Q475 reads: \"A 55-year-old female patient came complaining of headache, blurring of vision & redness in the right eye since one week … BCVA was 6/6 in right eye with +4 sphere & 6/6 in left eye with +4 sphere, right eye was quiet, anterior chamber was shallow, pupil was briskly reactive & IOP was 12 mmhg\". Its findings are restated above; its *diagnosis* is not, since that is Q475's own answer.",
    objective:'After a resolved intermittent angle-closure episode the next step is gonioscopy — to see whether the angle is merely narrow, in which case laser iridotomy suffices, or already sealed by peripheral anterior synechiae, which needs filtering surgery.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.73 (Q476), key p.78' },

  { id:'opqb-t11-477', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-glauc',
    stem:'A patient aged **50 years receiving antiglaucoma treatment** was advised to **undergo surgery**. This is because:',
    options:['Disc changes','Falling field','Angle is opened','PAS (peripheral anterior synechiae)'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"The most important indicator of glaucoma progression is **visual field changes, not disc changes as disc changes could sometimes happen without affecting the field and also due to the fact that minor disc changes could be missed and objectively determined by the ophthalmologist.**\n\n---\n\n*(The lines above are the bank's own printed explanation, transcribed verbatim. The notes below are added for study.)*\n\n**Why the field and not the pressure.** `L11) Glaucoma Diag, POAG` states the aim of treatment as \"**preserve visual function** to beyond the patient life expectancy at an affordable cost with the least side effects\". Visual function is the target, so visual function is the audit. Pressure is a proxy — the deck is explicit that IOP is \"a significant **risk factor**\", not the disease, and that eyes with normal pressure can still have the optic neuropathy (\"normal tension glaucoma\") while eyes with high pressure may have no disease at all (\"ocular hypertension\"). A treated eye whose field is still falling is failing treatment whatever its pressure reads.\n\n**Why the field and not the disc.** The bank's box gives two reasons and both are sound. The disc is judged by eye, from an appearance, and small changes in rim width are genuinely difficult to call from one visit to the next; a field is a measurement, plotted and comparable. `L11`'s own structural description — \"Focal → **polar notch**\" or \"Circumferential → generalized thinning\" — describes changes that can be subtle in the early stages and that a different examiner may score differently.\n\n**The three-stage escalation, from `L11`'s protocol slide.**\n\n| Stage | What it is |\n|---|---|\n| **Medical** | \"Decreasing aqueous humour production → beta-blockers, carbonic anhydrase inhibitors, alpha agonists. Increasing aqueous humour drainage → prostaglandin analogues, miotics, alpha agonists\" |\n| **Laser** | \"Laser trabeculoplasty (Argon, Selective)\" |\n| **Surgery** | \"Creation of a fistula between the anterior chamber and the subconjunctival space (**trabeculectomy**)\" |\n\nThe patient in the stem is already on medical treatment. Surgery is offered when the medical stage has demonstrably failed — and what demonstrates failure is a field that is still shrinking.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong.**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Disc changes** | ⚠️ a *real* sign of progression, and in some clinics the trigger. But the box explicitly ranks it below the field, for the two reasons quoted above. This is the near-miss distractor, and the bank's argument for rejecting it is printed. |\n| **C. Angle is opened** | an open angle is the normal state in POAG. It is a description of the disease, not evidence that it is worsening. |\n| **D. PAS (peripheral anterior synechiae)** | belongs to *angle closure*. `L12` makes them the lesion of chronic closure and `L1,2` slide 24 photographs them. They would indeed force a change of plan — Q464 and Q476 both turn on that — but not in a patient with open angle glaucoma on medical treatment. |\n\n**The same principle, three more times in this topic.** Q456 keys the peripheral field as what glaucoma attacks first. Q492 keys \"regular field plotting to follow up the progression of field changes\" as the safest monitoring test. Q497 keys \"a visual fields examination must be ordered first\" before starting treatment at all. **The field is the instrument.**",
    objective:'A falling visual field is the most reliable evidence that glaucoma is progressing on treatment, and therefore the indication for surgery — disc change is subjective and can occur without functional loss, while pressure is only a risk factor.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.74 (Q477), key p.78' },

  { id:'opqb-t11-478', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-glauc',
    stem:'The **Definitive management** of a **cataractous patient with a shallow anterior chamber** who **suddenly complains of pain & headache** is:',
    options:['Medical treatment','Paracentesis','Lens extraction','Trabeculectomy'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"This is probably a case of **phacomorphic glaucoma,** one of the secondary lens-induced glaucoma; the cataractous lens becomes larger in size with time blocking the pupil and causing acute angle closure. **Removing the lens in any lens-induced glaucoma is the treatment.**\n\n---\n\n*(The lines above are the bank's own printed explanation, transcribed verbatim. The notes below are added for study.)*\n\n**The mechanism, from the cataract decks.** `L17) Cataract I` lists among the complications of cataract: \"Intumescence (lens fibres liquefaction and fluid imbibition → **swelling of lens**)\" and \"Lens induced glaucoma → **Phacomorphic** → Intumescence (lens fibres fluid imbibition) → **increased lens thickness → pupil block → elevated IOP**\". `L18) Cataract II`'s table adds the picture — \"Phacomorphic Glaucoma — Immature cataract — Swollen lens → angle closure — **Red eye + pain + HM** — Raised IOP, corneal oedema — **Urgent phaco**\" — and its own exam slide puts lens-induced glaucoma in the \"**URGENT / MANDATORY**\" surgical column rather than the elective one.\n\nThe stem gives the three elements: a cataract (the swelling lens), a shallow anterior chamber (the diaphragm already pushed forward), and sudden pain with headache (the pressure spike). `L12) Glaucoma 2 Cong, PACG, 2ry` supplies the symptom set that pain-and-headache stands for — \"Pain → ++++ · Coloured haloes around light · Diminution of vision → rapid · Redness · **Nausea / Vomiting (D.Dx: Acute Abdomen)**\".\n\n**Why lens extraction and not the other three.** The word in the stem is **definitive**.\n\n| Option | Role | Why it is not definitive |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A. Medical treatment** | essential *first* | it lowers the pressure so the cornea clears and an operation becomes possible — `L12`'s list is acetazolamide, topical beta-blockers, alpha agonists, CAIs, miotics, mannitol, glycerol, steroids. But the lens keeps swelling. Medicine buys hours, not a cure. |\n| **B. Paracentesis** | a decompression manoeuvre | drains the anterior chamber to drop pressure fast. Temporary by definition — the chamber refills, and the lens is still there. |\n| **C. Lens extraction** | **the key** | removes the *cause*. Once the fat lens is out, the chamber deepens, the pupil block ends and the angle reopens. `L18`: \"Urgent phaco\". |\n| **D. Trabeculectomy** | a fistula | `L11` describes it as \"Creation of a fistula between the anterior chamber and the subconjunctival space\". It bypasses an angle that has failed permanently. Here the angle is mechanically obstructed by something removable — building a new drain while leaving the obstruction in place is the wrong operation. |\n\n**⚠️ Why pilocarpine is not the answer either.** In *primary* pupil block a miotic helps by pulling the peripheral iris out of the angle. Against a swollen lens it can make things worse, because miosis tightens the iris against the anterior lens capsule. Q463 in this topic keys precisely that: \"It responds well to pilocarpine\" is false for phacomorphic glaucoma.\n\n**The general rule the box states.** *Any* lens-induced glaucoma — phacomorphic from a swollen immature lens, phacolytic from a leaking hypermature one — is treated by removing the lens. Q489 keys cataract extraction for the phacolytic form on exactly this principle.\n\n*(Secondary chapter: `op-cat` — the mechanism and the urgency are taught in `L17` and `L18`.)*",
    objective:'A cataract with a shallow chamber and sudden pain is phacomorphic glaucoma; medical treatment and paracentesis only buy time, and the definitive management of any lens-induced glaucoma is removal of the lens.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.74 (Q478), key p.78' },

  { id:'opqb-t11-479', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-glauc',
    stem:'What is the **value of this instrument**?',
    options:['Measure IOP','Measure VA','See anterior chamber','All of the above'],
    answer:2,
    imgAlt:'Colour clinical close-up photograph: a hand holds a small ring-shaped ophthalmic instrument up in front of a patient’s eye, the patient’s other eye at the edge of the frame.',
    explanation:"**The instrument is a goniolens — a mirrored contact lens used to examine the anterior chamber angle.**\n\nThe photograph shows a Goldmann-type **three-mirror gonioscopy lens** held against the eye; the three angled mirrors set inside the ring are what give it away. `L11) Glaucoma Diag, POAG` and `L12` both define what it is for in the same sentence: \"**Gonioscopy → visualization of the angle of the anterior chamber**\".\n\n**What it shows.** `L1,2) Ocular Anatomy, Symptomatology & Basic Eye Examination` teaches the view on slides 22–24 with the mnemonic \"**I CAN'T SEE THE STUPID LINE**\", read outwards from the iris — **I**ris, **C**iliary body, **S**cleral spur, **T**rabecular meshwork, **S**chwalbe's **L**ine — and photographs the recess itself, captioned \"Open Angle ⇢ Narrow Angle ⇢ Closed Angle\", with a further slide showing peripheral anterior synechiae. Q467 in this topic asks which structure is *not* on that list.\n\n**Why the angle cannot be seen without it.** Light leaving the angle meets the front surface of the cornea beyond the critical angle and is totally internally reflected, so the recess never emerges from the eye. Placing a contact lens on the cornea removes that interface, and an internal mirror then turns the line of sight across the chamber so the observer can look into the opposite recess. *(The optics are standard and are **not taken from the course material**; the decks state what gonioscopy is, not why a lens is needed.)*\n\n**Why the other three are wrong.**\n\n| Option | Instrument that actually does it |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Measure IOP** | a tonometer — Goldmann applanation or a Schiötz indentation tonometer. Both also touch the cornea, which is why this is the strongest distractor; but a goniolens has no measuring mechanism at all. |\n| **B. Measure VA** | a Snellen or LogMAR chart, at a set distance. Nothing to do with a contact lens. |\n| **D. All of the above** | fails with A and B. |\n\n**Why the answer is worded \"see anterior chamber\" rather than \"see the angle\".** The angle *is* part of the anterior chamber — its peripheral recess, where iris meets cornea. The bank's phrasing is loose but points at the right structure, and it is the only option in the list that names a viewing function rather than a measuring one.\n\n⚠️ **A note on the same lens's other uses.** A Goldmann three-mirror lens is also used to examine the peripheral retina and the vitreous, because its other two mirrors are angled for that. The bank does not offer that option, and the decks do not mention it, so it is not in play here — but it is why the instrument has three mirrors and not one. **Not taken from the course material.**\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'A mirrored contact lens held on the cornea is a goniolens; it is used for gonioscopy — visualising the anterior chamber angle — and not for measuring pressure or acuity.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.74 (Q479), key p.78' },

  { id:'opqb-t11-480', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-glauc',
    stem:'What type of ocular disease can occur in **the following photo**?',
    options:['Retinal detachment','Open angle glaucoma','Normal tension glaucoma','AACG'],
    answer:3,
    imgAlt:'Slit-lamp photograph of a brown eye with an oblique orange slit beam crossing the cornea and falling on the iris.',
    explanation:"**The slit beam shows a very shallow anterior chamber, and a shallow chamber is the anatomy that permits an acute attack of angle closure glaucoma.**\n\nThe photograph is a slit-lamp view with the beam thrown obliquely across the cornea. What the beam demonstrates is the *depth* of the chamber: the section of the beam on the cornea and the section of it on the iris lie almost against each other, with barely any dark gap between them. In a normal eye those two sections are separated by a visible space — the aqueous-filled chamber. Here there is almost none.\n\n**Why that anatomy points to AACG.** `L12) Glaucoma 2 Cong, PACG, 2ry` builds the acute attack on a crowded anterior segment. Its risk profile — \"Who is at risk for angle closure glaucoma? (**crowded anterior segments**)\" — lists \"Females · Hyperopes · Race (South-East Asians) · Advancing age · Affected fellow eye\", and its sign list for the attack itself includes \"**Shallow anterior chamber / Iris bombe (forward bowing)**\". `L7,8) Refractive errors` states the same predisposition from the refractive side: hyperopia carries a \"**Predisposition to narrow angle glaucoma due to shallow anterior chamber** (especially later in life as the lens becomes thicker)\".\n\n**Why the other three are wrong.**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Retinal detachment** | a posterior segment disease. Nothing about it is visible on a slit-lamp view of the anterior segment, and a slit beam on the cornea and iris cannot reach it. |\n| **B. Open angle glaucoma** | ⚠️ the anterior segment in POAG is **normal**. `L11) Glaucoma Diag, POAG` defines the open angle as one where the meshwork is \"visibly exposed to the aqueous humour\", and the chamber depth is unremarkable. A photograph showing a shallow chamber is evidence *against* this option, not for it. |\n| **C. Normal tension glaucoma** | `L11` lists it as an *exception* to the triad — \"eyes with optic neuropathy and **normal IOP**\". It is a disc and field diagnosis, made on perimetry and optic nerve examination, with a normal anterior segment. Nothing in an anterior segment photograph can suggest it. |\n\n**The general lesson.** Two of the three wrong answers are glaucomas, and the photograph distinguishes them by anterior segment anatomy alone. That is the practical value of the oblique-torch or slit-beam estimate of chamber depth: **it is a bedside test for whether an eye is dilatable.** Q450 and Q459 both key atropine as the drug that will precipitate an attack in exactly this eye, and Q485 keys the hypermetropic eye as the one at risk.\n\n⚠️ **The photograph is essential to this question** — the stem names no finding at all. Note also that this is a *photograph of anatomy*, not of an attack in progress: the eye is white, the pupil is not obviously mid-dilated, and no corneal oedema is convincingly visible. The stem's wording is careful about that — it asks what disease \"**can occur**\" in such an eye, not what the eye is suffering from now.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'An oblique slit beam showing the corneal and iris sections almost touching indicates a very shallow anterior chamber — the crowded anterior segment that predisposes to acute angle closure glaucoma, and which is normal in open angle and normal tension glaucoma.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.74 (Q480), key p.78' },

  { id:'opqb-t11-481', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-glauc',
    stem:'In **acute angel closure glaucoma**, the **pupil** is:',
    options:['Miotic non-reactive.','Festooned, non-reactive.','Mid-dilated, non-reactive.','D shaped, reactive.'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**`L12) Glaucoma 2 Cong, PACG, 2ry` prints the answer as one of its signs, in these words: \"Mid-dilated vertically oval non-reactive pupil\".**\n\n*(The stem prints \"acute **angel** closure glaucoma\"; the word is **angle**. A transcription of the misprint, not a repair of the medicine.)*\n\n**Why mid-dilated and not fully dilated, and not small.** The pupil position is not incidental to the attack — it *is* the attack. `L12`'s pathogenesis chain opens with it: \"**Mid-dilated position of the pupil** → relative pupil block → accumulation of aqueous humour behind the iris → iris bombe → narrowing of the angle of the anterior chamber → increased resistance to aqueous humour outflow → accumulation of aqueous humour in the eye → **elevation of IOP (Rapid severe rise of IOP)**\".\n\nAt the mid position two things are simultaneously true: the iris lies against the anterior lens capsule over a broad ring, maximising the resistance aqueous meets on its way through the pupil; and the peripheral iris is loose enough to bunch into the angle recess when it balloons forward. A widely dilated pupil stretches the iris taut and off the lens; a constricted one holds it flat and thin. Only the intermediate position does both. This is why `L12` lists \"**Dark environment**\" and \"Use of systemic medications (**anticholinergics**)\" as things that increase the risk — both put the pupil there.\n\n**Why it is non-reactive.** The pressure in an acute attack is \"IOP → **++++ (stony hard)**\". At those pressures the iris sphincter becomes ischaemic and simply stops working, so the pupil is fixed where the attack caught it. That fixity often persists after the attack is broken.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong.**\n\n| Option | Whose pupil it is |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Miotic non-reactive** | **acute anterior uveitis** — ciliary spasm and sphincter irritation make the pupil small. Q473 in this topic keys uveitis on exactly that finding, at an IOP of 27 rather than a stony-hard one. |\n| **B. Festooned, non-reactive** | uveitis again, at a later stage. `L13,14) Uvea & Sclera` photographs \"pupils grossly distorted into festooned/irregular shapes by **posterior synechiae**\" — the scalloped outline of an iris stuck to the lens at intervals. |\n| **D. D shaped, reactive** | a **sector iridectomy** or a traumatic iridodialysis — a mechanical defect in the iris, not a physiological state. It has nothing to do with pressure, and \"reactive\" contradicts the attack outright. |\n\n**The shape the deck adds and the bank omits.** `L12` says the pupil is *vertically oval* as well as mid-dilated. Q470 in this topic prints \"Dilated vertically oval pupil\" as one of its true findings. The oval shape comes from uneven ischaemia of the sphincter and is worth recognising, though this stem does not test it.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'In acute angle closure the pupil is mid-dilated, vertically oval and non-reactive — the mid position is what produces pupil block, and the sphincter is paralysed by the pressure.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.74 (Q481), key p.78' },

  { id:'opqb-t11-482', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-glauc',
    stem:'A **female aged 50 years old** suddenly developed **severe headache, drop of vision** [in] **one eye**. We should search for **diagnostic sign** in:',
    options:['The cornea','The pupil','The lens','The fundus'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"In acute angle-closure, **pupil reaction and shape are key diagnostic signs.**\n\n---\n\n*(The line above is the bank's own printed explanation, transcribed verbatim. The notes below are added for study.)*\n\n*(The bank prints \"drop of vision one eye\", omitting \"in\". The word is supplied in brackets above; the printed text is \"**drop of vision one eye**\".)*\n\n**Why the pupil.** `L12) Glaucoma 2 Cong, PACG, 2ry` lists the signs of an acute attack — \"Diminution of vision · IOP → ++++ (stony hard) · Corneal oedema — (Fundus examination, gonioscopy → usually not possible) · Ciliary injection · **Mid-dilated vertically oval non-reactive pupil** · Shallow anterior chamber / Iris bombe · Eyelid oedema\" — and of that list, the pupil is the one sign that is *specific*, *visible without instruments*, and *available in this eye*.\n\nSpecific, because a mid-dilated, vertically oval, fixed pupil belongs to angle closure and to almost nothing else: uveitis gives a small or festooned pupil (Q473, Q481), and an optic nerve or retinal cause of sudden visual loss gives a relative afferent defect in a pupil of normal size and shape. Visible, because it needs only a torch in a woman who is in too much pain to cooperate with much else.\n\n**Why the other three fail — and two of them fail for the same reason.**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **A. The cornea** | it *is* abnormal — `L12` lists corneal oedema — but oedema is non-specific. It occurs after any pressure spike, in some keratitides, and in endothelial disease. It tells you the eye is in trouble, not which trouble. |\n| **C. The lens** | invisible through an oedematous cornea, and unchanged by the attack in any case. A cataract, if present, is incidental to the acute picture. |\n| **D. The fundus** | ⚠️ `L12` rules it out explicitly: \"(Fundus examination, gonioscopy → **usually not possible**)\", because the cornea is cloudy. Even in an eye you could see into, the disc changes of glaucoma take months. This distractor is the most tempting for a patient with sudden visual loss, which is why the deck's parenthesis matters. |\n\n**The wider clinical point.** Sudden monocular visual loss with severe headache in a 50-year-old has a short and dangerous differential — acute angle closure, giant cell arteritis, and a posterior circulation event among them — and the first three are told apart at the front of the eye. `L12` warns about one of the confusions in its own symptom list: \"**Nausea / Vomiting (D.Dx: Acute Abdomen)**\" — patients with an acute attack have been operated on for a surgical abdomen. Looking at the pupil is what prevents that.\n\n**Q483 is the same patient one question later**, with the halos and the red painful eye spelled out, and it asks the treatment.",
    objective:'In sudden headache with monocular visual loss the pupil is the diagnostic sign to seek: a mid-dilated, vertically oval, fixed pupil identifies acute angle closure, where the cornea is only non-specifically oedematous and the fundus cannot be seen.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.74 (Q482), key p.78' },

  { id:'opqb-t11-483', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-glauc',
    stem:'A **female, 50 years old**, was complaining of **halos of light after being in the dark**, suddenly developed **severe headache, drop of vision and red painful eye**. The **valuable local treatment** is:',
    options:['Antibiotics','Miotics','Corticosteroids','Mydriatics'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**\"Local\" means topical, and the topical drug that reverses the mechanism of this attack is a miotic — pilocarpine.**\n\n**The history is `L12`'s prodrome followed by `L12`'s crisis.** `L12) Glaucoma 2 Cong, PACG, 2ry` describes the intermittent stage as \"Short episodes of angle closure · **Coloured haloes around light** · eye pain, brow ache or headache · Attacks resolve by **bright illumination** and sleep\", and lists \"**Dark environment**\" among the things that increase risk. Halos in the dark are the pupil drifting to mid-dilation and the cornea beginning to swell. The acute attack that followed is the deck's next station: \"Pain → ++++ · Coloured haloes around light · Diminution of vision → rapid (over several hours) · **Redness** · Nausea / Vomiting\".\n\n**Why a miotic.** `L12`'s medical treatment of the acute attack is a list of six agents plus hyperosmotics: \"Systemic acetazolamide · Topical beta-blockers · Topical alpha agonists · Topical CAIs · **Topical miotics (pilocarpine)**\" and \"Hyperosmotic agents: IV mannitol, Oral hypertonic glycerol · Topical steroids\". Of these, the miotic is the only one that acts on the *mechanism* rather than on the volume of aqueous: constricting the pupil pulls the peripheral iris out of the angle recess and off the trabecular meshwork, and `L11) Glaucoma Diag, POAG` puts \"miotics\" on the drainage-increasing line. Every other topical agent on the list simply turns the tap down.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong.**\n\n| Option | Effect here |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Antibiotics** | the eye is red from ciliary injection under pressure, not from infection. There is no discharge, no infiltrate, nothing to treat. |\n| **C. Corticosteroids** | ⚠️ `L12` **does** list \"Topical steroids\" in the acute regimen — they settle the intense inflammation the pressure spike provokes. But they are adjunctive; they do not open the angle. And `L11` lists \"Steroid induced\" among the causes of secondary open angle glaucoma, so they are never the *valuable* agent in a pressure emergency. |\n| **D. Mydriatics** | ⚠️⚠️ **the catastrophic answer.** Dilating this pupil takes it to the mid position that produces pupil block. Q450 keys atropine as contraindicated in primary angle closure and Q459 keys it as the drug that precipitates the attack. Giving a mydriatic here would deepen the crisis. |\n\n**Where the miotic sits in the sequence.** Medicine first — `L12` even begins the list with \"**Hospitalisation**\" — then, once the pressure is down and the cornea clears, definitive surgery: \"Iridotomy — **Laser (standard)**, Surgical\" or filtering surgery. Q491 keys the *first* line as a carbonic anhydrase inhibitor for a patient in the same state, and Q493 keys examining the angle before choosing the operation. The three questions are one algorithm: **break the attack, then read the angle, then operate.**\n\n⚠️ **A caveat worth knowing.** At very high pressures the iris sphincter is ischaemic and pilocarpine may not work until the pressure has been lowered by other means — which is one reason `L12` lists systemic acetazolamide and mannitol alongside it. *(That miotics are ineffective at extreme pressures is **not taken from the course material**; `L12` lists the agents without ranking them.)*\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Halos in the dark followed by a red painful eye with headache and visual loss is acute angle closure; the valuable topical agent is a miotic, which pulls the peripheral iris out of the angle — a mydriatic would worsen the attack.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.74 (Q483), key p.78' },

  { id:'opqb-t11-484', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-glauc',
    stem:'Which of the following **intraocular pressure lowering medications** are having the **strongest therapeutic effect** in lowering intraocular pressure?',
    options:['Prostaglandins analogues','Beta blockers','Pilocarpine','Mannitol'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**Mannitol lowers pressure harder and faster than anything else on the list, because it works by a different physical mechanism entirely.**\n\n`L12) Glaucoma 2 Cong, PACG, 2ry` files it separately from the drug classes for exactly that reason. Its acute treatment slide reads: \"IOP lowering Rx → Systemic acetazolamide · Topical beta-blockers · Topical alpha agonists · Topical CAIs · Topical miotics (pilocarpine)\" and then, as its own heading, \"**Hyperosmotic agents → IV mannitol · Oral hypertonic glycerol**\".\n\n**Why a hyperosmotic is in a class of its own.** Every other agent on the list adjusts a *rate* — how fast aqueous is made, or how fast it leaves. `L11) Glaucoma Diag, POAG` sorts them into those two columns: \"Decreasing aqueous humour production → beta-blockers, carbonic anhydrase inhibitors, alpha agonists. Increasing aqueous humour drainage → prostaglandin analogues, miotics, alpha agonists.\" Rates take hours to change a volume. Mannitol changes the volume directly: given intravenously it raises plasma osmolality, and water is drawn osmotically out of the vitreous across the blood–ocular barrier. The eye physically shrinks, and the pressure falls within minutes. *(The osmotic gradient across the blood–ocular barrier is the standard explanation and is **not taken from the course material**; `L12` names the agent and its route, not its mechanism.)*\n\n**⚠️ Note what the key is and is not saying.** Mannitol is **not** a treatment for chronic glaucoma. It is intravenous, short-acting, and carries real systemic risks — fluid overload in a cardiac patient, renal load, electrolyte shift. It appears in `L12` only in the emergency setting, and `L11`'s POAG protocol — medical, then laser, then trabeculectomy — never mentions it. Q497 in this topic offers \"mannitol or IV acetazolamide before discharging him\" as a *wrong* answer for a quiet ocular hypertensive, and that is the same fact from the other side: **strongest is not the same as most appropriate.** The stem here asks only which lowers pressure most, and among these four it is mannitol.\n\n**Why the other three are weaker.**\n\n| Option | Class and route | Where it ranks |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A. Prostaglandins analogues** | topical, increases outflow (`L11`'s drainage line) | the most effective *topical* agent in routine use, and the usual first-line drop — which is what makes it the strong distractor. Still a rate change, still hours. |\n| **B. Beta blockers** | topical, reduces production (`L11`'s production line) | long the standard first-line agent; less effective than prostaglandin analogues. |\n| **C. Pilocarpine** | topical miotic, increases trabecular outflow (`L11`'s drainage line) | valuable in angle closure because of what it does to the *iris* rather than the size of its pressure drop; Q483 keys it for that reason. |\n\n⚠️ **That prostaglandin analogues outperform beta-blockers, and that both are outperformed acutely by hyperosmotics, is a ranking the decks do not state.** `L11` lists the classes without comparing their potency. The ordering above is **not taken from the course material**; what *is* taught is the separation `L12` draws by giving hyperosmotics their own heading in the emergency protocol.\n\n**Shared table:** the production/drainage split is written out in `opqb-t11-457`.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Hyperosmotic agents such as IV mannitol lower intraocular pressure most powerfully because they osmotically remove water from the vitreous rather than adjusting the rate of aqueous production or outflow — but they are emergency, not maintenance, therapy.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.75 (Q484), key p.78' },

  { id:'opqb-t11-485', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-glauc',
    stem:'The eye susceptible to **AACG**',
    options:['Hypermetropic eye','Myopic eye','Astigmatic eye','Aphakic eye'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**Hyperopic** eyes have **shorter axial lengths and shallow AC,** predisposing to AACG.\n\n---\n\n*(The line above is the bank's own printed explanation, transcribed verbatim. The notes below are added for study.)*\n\n**Both sides of the module teach this, from opposite directions.** `L7,8) Refractive errors` gives hyperopia's complications, and the first one is: \"**Predisposition to narrow angle glaucoma due to shallow anterior chamber** (especially later in life as the lens becomes thicker)\". `L12) Glaucoma 2 Cong, PACG, 2ry` lists who is at risk of angle closure under the heading \"**crowded anterior segments**\": \"Females · **Hyperopes** · Race (South-East Asians) · Advancing age · Affected fellow eye\".\n\n**The geometry.** A hyperopic eye is short — the whole globe, front to back. A short globe has a shallower anterior chamber and a proportionally larger lens sitting closer to the cornea, so the angle recess is narrow from the outset. `L7,8`'s parenthesis adds the time axis: the lens keeps growing throughout life, so the chamber shallows further with each decade. That is why the disease is one of the fifth decade and beyond rather than of youth, and why `L12` lists \"Advancing age\" as a separate risk alongside hyperopia.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong.**\n\n| Option | Anatomy | Effect on the angle |\n|---|---|---|\n| **B. Myopic eye** | long globe, deep anterior chamber, wide angle | the *protective* refractive state for angle closure. ⚠️ Note that myopia is a risk factor for the **other** glaucoma: `L11) Glaucoma Diag, POAG` lists \"Refractive state → more common in **myopia**\" among the predisposing factors for primary **open** angle glaucoma. The two refractive errors sort the two glaucomas — a fact worth carrying as a pair. |\n| **C. Astigmatic eye** | irregular corneal or lenticular curvature | astigmatism is about the shape of a refracting surface, not the depth of the chamber. It has no bearing on the angle. |\n| **D. Aphakic eye** | the lens has been removed | the opposite of crowded. Taking the lens out deepens the anterior chamber dramatically — which is exactly why lens extraction is the *cure* for phacomorphic glaucoma (Q478) and why cataract surgery is protective against angle closure. |\n\n**The clinical corollary.** A middle-aged hyperope with a shallow chamber is the patient in whom the pupil must not be dilated casually, and the patient whose fellow eye is treated prophylactically after a first attack. Q475's vignette is exactly this eye — \"6/6 in right eye with **+4 sphere** & 6/6 in left eye with +4 sphere … anterior chamber was shallow\" — and Q449 keys the fellow-eye iridotomy.\n\n*(Secondary chapter: `op-refract` — `L7,8` slide 58 is where the predisposition is taught as a complication of hyperopia.)*",
    objective:'The hypermetropic eye is short, with a shallow anterior chamber and a crowded angle that shallows further as the lens thickens with age — the anatomy that permits acute angle closure; myopia predisposes instead to open angle glaucoma.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.75 (Q485), key p.78' },

  { id:'opqb-t11-486', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-glauc',
    stem:'**Argon laser trabeculoplasty** is indicated in:',
    options:['Chronic congestive glaucoma','Neovascular glaucoma','Chronic simple glaucoma','Congenital glaucoma'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"Argon laser trabeculoplasty **(ALT) is indicated for primary open-angle** (chronic simple) glaucoma to **improve trabecular outflow.**\n\n---\n\n*(The line above is the bank's own printed explanation, transcribed verbatim. The notes below are added for study.)*\n\n**\"Chronic simple glaucoma\" is the old name for POAG**, and `L11) Glaucoma Diag, POAG` is where the laser appears. Its treatment protocol has three stages: \"Medical treatment → …; **Laser treatment → Laser trabeculoplasty (Argon, Selective)**; Surgery → Creation of a fistula between the anterior chamber and the subconjunctival space (trabeculectomy)\". Trabeculoplasty is named once in all thirty cached decks, and it is on that line.\n\n**Why the angle has to be open.** The laser burns are placed *on the trabecular meshwork itself*, through a goniolens. That requires the meshwork to be visible — which is `L11`'s own definition of an open angle: \"outflow route (trabecular meshwork) **visibly exposed** to the aqueous humour\". You cannot treat what you cannot see, and you cannot treat a meshwork that an iris is lying on. The effect is to increase outflow through the existing drain, which fits `L11`'s pathology for POAG: \"Increased resistance to aqueous humour drainage → trabecular meshwork → Schlemm's canal → collector channels\".\n\n**Why the other three are wrong, and each for a different structural reason.**\n\n| Option | The angle in that disease | Why ALT fails |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A. Chronic congestive glaucoma** | the old name for **chronic angle closure**. `L12` gives it \"Repeated episodes of intermittent / acute angle closure attacks → **chronic closure of the angle**\" with peripheral anterior synechiae. | the meshwork is covered by iris. Nothing to aim at. |\n| **B. Neovascular glaucoma** | `L12`: a neovascular membrane \"grows over the trabecular meshwork and contracts pulling the peripheral iris over the trabeculum\". | the meshwork is under a fibrovascular sheet and the angle is being pulled shut. Lasering it would bleed and achieve nothing. |\n| **D. Congenital glaucoma** | `L12`: \"Persistent mesodermal membrane on trabecular meshwork (goniodysgenesis) · Anterior iris insertion obscuring trabecular meshwork · **Absent Schlemm's canal**\". | the meshwork is malformed and the canal may not exist. `L12` treats it by **cutting** the tissue — goniotomy or trabeculotomy — not by lasering it. Q460 keys goniotomy. |\n\n**The naming, since two of the options are old names for real diseases.**\n\n| Old name | Modern name |\n|---|---|\n| Chronic **simple** glaucoma | primary **open** angle glaucoma |\n| Chronic **congestive** glaucoma | chronic **angle closure** glaucoma |\n| Acute congestive glaucoma | acute angle closure glaucoma (Q487 uses this one) |\n\n⚠️ **These synonyms are not in either glaucoma deck.** `L11` and `L12` use only the modern terms; the bank uses the older ones across several questions in this topic. The equivalences above are **not taken from the course material** — but they are needed to answer, so they are recorded here rather than left as a gap.\n\n**Where the laser sits.** `L11`'s protocol is an escalation, and the laser occupies the rung between drops and a filtering operation. Q477 keys a falling field as the reason to climb it.",
    objective:'Argon laser trabeculoplasty treats primary open angle (chronic simple) glaucoma by improving outflow through a visible trabecular meshwork; it is useless where the angle is closed, covered by a neovascular membrane, or congenitally malformed.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.75 (Q486), key p.78' },

  { id:'opqb-t11-487', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-glauc',
    stem:'Which type of glaucoma can cause **gradual painless loss of visual functions**?',
    options:['Acute congestive glaucoma','Primary open angle glaucoma','Buphthalmos','Neovascular glaucoma'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**POAG is the silent glaucoma, and `L11` says so in one word.**\n\n`L11) Glaucoma Diag, POAG` gives the presentation of primary open angle glaucoma as: \"Most commonly → **asymptomatic**. Less commonly → headache (early morning), frequent change of reading glasses. Advanced disease → **peripheral visual field loss, diminution of vision**\".\n\nThat is the whole answer. There is no pain because there is no acute pressure spike, no corneal oedema and no ciliary spasm — the pressure rises slowly enough that the eye accommodates to it. And there is no early visual symptom because, as `L11`'s field list shows, the damage starts in the periphery and the paracentral zone: \"Paracentral scotomata in Bjerrum's area · Arcuate scotomata · Nasal step · Ring scotoma · Constriction of visual field (→ tubular field) · Temporal island of vision\". Central acuity is confined to \"Diminution of central vision **in end stage disease**\". A patient with one good eye and an unnoticed nasal step has no symptom to report.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong — all three announce themselves.**\n\n| Option | How it presents |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Acute congestive glaucoma** | the old name for acute angle closure. `L12) Glaucoma 2 Cong, PACG, 2ry`: \"Pain → **++++** · Coloured haloes around light · Diminution of vision → **rapid (over several hours)** · Redness · Nausea / Vomiting\". Sudden and agonising — the opposite of the stem on both counts. |\n| **C. Buphthalmos** | congenital glaucoma. `L12`'s symptoms are \"**Photophobia, watering, blepharospasm** · Large eye · Abnormal colour of the eye · Diminution of vision\" — a distressed, photophobic infant whose eye is visibly abnormal. Neither gradual in the adult sense nor painless. |\n| **D. Neovascular glaucoma** | `L12` files it under secondary narrow angle glaucoma; `L18` and the clinical picture give it a red, painful eye with very high pressure. `L19.2) Retinal Vascular Disorders` calls the prognosis of its usual cause \"**Very poor**\". Painful, and superimposed on an eye that has already lost vision from the underlying retinal disease. |\n\n**Why the silence is the danger.** `L11` opens its treatment slide with \"Glaucoma is a **potentially blinding disease**, referral to an ophthalmologist is a must\" — and the reason a slow, painless disease needs that warning is that nothing brings the patient in. By the time \"diminution of vision\" appears on `L11`'s presentation list, the disease is already \"advanced\". This is the argument for screening the disc and the field in anyone over 40, and Q453 keys the disc as the diagnostic finding.\n\n⚠️ **Q488 is this question inverted**, one number later on the same page: \"Painful red eyes do not occur with:\", keying primary open angle glaucoma. Two stems, one fact, adjacent — flagged for the sweep in the staging file.\n\n*(The stem is printed without a question mark; a mark is supplied above.)*\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Primary open angle glaucoma is asymptomatic until advanced — no pain because the pressure rises slowly, and no early visual complaint because the loss begins in the peripheral and paracentral field.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.75 (Q487), key p.78' },

  { id:'opqb-t11-488', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-glauc',
    stem:'**Painful red eyes** do **not** occur with:',
    options:['Primary open angle glaucoma','Primary angle closure glaucoma','Secondary angle closure glaucoma','Neovascular glaucoma'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**Pain and redness in glaucoma come from a rapid pressure spike, and only the open angle disease rises slowly enough to avoid one.**\n\n`L11) Glaucoma Diag, POAG` gives POAG's presentation as \"Most commonly → **asymptomatic**\", with headache and frequent change of reading glasses as the *less* common complaints and visual loss confined to advanced disease. Redness is not on the list at all, because the pressure never rises fast enough to congest the ciliary vessels or to swell the cornea.\n\nThe other three all do.\n\n| Option | Why it hurts |\n|---|---|\n| **B. Primary angle closure glaucoma** | `L12) Glaucoma 2 Cong, PACG, 2ry`: \"Pain → **++++** · Coloured haloes · Diminution of vision → rapid · **Redness** · Nausea / Vomiting\", with signs including \"**Ciliary injection**\" and \"IOP → ++++ (stony hard)\". |\n| **C. Secondary angle closure glaucoma** | the same mechanical closure with a visible cause. `L12` lists neovascular glaucoma, iridocorneal endothelial syndrome, intraocular tumours and retinal vein occlusion; `L18) Cataract II` adds phacomorphic glaucoma with \"**Red eye + pain + HM**\". |\n| **D. Neovascular glaucoma** | a secondary closure by \"anterior pulling\" of a fibrovascular membrane, and among the most painful eyes in ophthalmology once the angle has sealed. |\n\n**Where the pain actually comes from.** Not from the pressure as a number, but from the rate of change. A sudden rise stretches the corneal epithelium into bullae, congests the anterior ciliary vessels — `L12`'s \"ciliary injection\" — and provokes ciliary spasm; the trigeminal supply to all three then reports it as severe deep pain, often referred over the brow and accompanied by vomiting (`L12` warns \"D.Dx: **Acute Abdomen**\"). A pressure that climbs over years does none of this, which is why an eye at 32 mmHg for a decade can be entirely comfortable.\n\n**⚠️ The one exception the decks record.** `L12`'s continuum ends at \"**Absolute glaucoma**\", annotated \"**Blind painful eye**\". So an untreated open angle glaucoma can, at the very end, arrive at pain — but it does so having become absolute, not as a feature of the disease the question is asking about.\n\n**⚠️ Q487 is this same question inverted**, one number earlier on the same page: \"Which type of glaucoma can cause gradual painless loss of visual functions\", keying primary open angle glaucoma. Same fact, adjacent numbers, opposite framing. Flagged for the sweep in the staging file. The clinical table above is written once, here and in `opqb-t11-487`, and the sibling entries point at them.\n\n**The screening consequence.** Because POAG neither hurts nor reddens, it cannot be found by waiting for a complaint. `L11` says \"Glaucoma is a potentially blinding disease, referral to an ophthalmologist is a must\" and makes the examination — disc, pressure, and secondary features — the route to the diagnosis. Q453, Q456 and Q477 are all built on that.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'A painful red eye in glaucoma comes from a rapid pressure rise, so it accompanies primary and secondary angle closure and neovascular glaucoma; primary open angle glaucoma is white and painless until it is advanced.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.75 (Q488), key p.78' },

  /* opqb-t11-489 WAS FOLDED, NOT WRITTEN -- 2026-08-24.
     The same question as `opqb-t9-351` (book p.54): same stem word for word
     INCLUDING THE BANK’S OWN TYPO "Phakolytic", same four options, same key.
     The only differences are a full stop on each option and "B blockers" for
     "Beta blockers" -- an abbreviation, not a replaced distractor.
     A SHARED TYPO IS STRONGER EVIDENCE OF A REPRINT THAN A SHARED ANSWER.
     This printing IS boxed and p.54’s is not, so the box was folded into the
     held entry and ITS AUTHORED MARKER CAME OFF -- the third time on this module.
     Held in `op-cat`, not `op-glauc`: the module teaches the lens-induced
     glaucomas in L17/L18, not in either glaucoma deck. */
  { id:'opqb-t11-490', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-glauc',
    stem:'A **45-year-old female** presented in the emergency room, with **pain for the last 5 hours** accompanied by **drop in vision in her right eye**. She reported having **one similar attack before** in the same eye. The pupil was **markedly constricted on the right side**. The **key of diagnosis** is:',
    options:['The intraocular pressure.','The angle of the anterior chamber.','The constituents of the anterior chamber.','The pupil in the fellow eye'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**The state of the angle is what names this disease, and what decides what is done about it.**\n\n`L11) Glaucoma Diag, POAG` and `L12) Glaucoma 2 Cong, PACG, 2ry` both make the angle the classifying observation: \"Gonioscopy → visualization of the angle of the anterior chamber. **Open angle** → outflow route (trabecular meshwork) visibly exposed to the aqueous humour. **Closed angle** → outflow route not visibly exposed\". Nothing else in this vignette can do that job.\n\n**Why the history points where it does.** \"One similar attack before in the same eye\" is `L12`'s intermittent stage: \"Short episodes of angle closure · Attacks resolve spontaneously · Mild symptoms … · Attacks resolve by bright illumination and sleep\", with \"**Affected fellow eye**\" listed as a risk factor and \"**Iridotomy — Laser, Surgical**\" printed as its treatment. A recurrent, self-limiting painful episode in one eye is angle closure until the angle proves otherwise.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong.**\n\n| Option | Why it does not settle the diagnosis |\n|---|---|\n| **A. The intraocular pressure** | tells you the outflow has failed, not *why*. `L11` is explicit that IOP is \"a significant **risk factor**\", not the disease, and that eyes exist with high pressure and no disease (ocular hypertension) and with normal pressure and disease (normal tension glaucoma). A high reading fits closure, uveitic glaucoma, phacomorphic glaucoma and neovascular glaucoma equally. |\n| **C. The constituents of the anterior chamber** | cells and flare would point at uveitis (Q473's eye), and this is the strongest distractor given the small pupil. But their *absence* would not exclude anything, and their presence occurs secondarily after any severe pressure spike. |\n| **D. The pupil in the fellow eye** | the fellow eye's **angle** matters enormously — `L12` lists \"Affected fellow eye\" among the risks and Q449 keys prophylactic iridotomy for it. Its *pupil* tells you nothing about this eye's mechanism. |\n\n**⚠️ The finding that does not fit, recorded and not corrected.** The stem says the pupil was \"**markedly constricted**\". `L12`'s sign list for an acute attack reads \"**Mid-dilated** vertically oval non-reactive pupil\", and Q481 in this same topic keys mid-dilated. A markedly constricted pupil is the uveitis pattern (Q473), not the closure pattern. Three readings are possible: the vignette is describing an eye already treated with a miotic in the emergency room — `L12` lists \"Topical miotics (pilocarpine)\" first among the topical agents; the wording is loose for the vertically oval pupil of an attack; or the detail is simply inconsistent with the intended diagnosis. **The key is not disputed** — and note that the key survives all three readings, because whichever way the pupil is read, the angle is still what settles the question. That is presumably why the bank asks for the \"key of diagnosis\" rather than the diagnosis itself.\n\n**What follows from looking.** Q464 keys gonioscopy as what chooses between iridectomy and a filtering operation; Q476 keys it as the next step after a prodrome; Q493 keys examining the angle after the attack subsides. **Four questions, one instruction: look at the angle.**\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'In a recurrent acute painful eye the diagnostic key is the state of the anterior chamber angle, since gonioscopy is what separates open from closed angle disease and selects the operation — pressure alone is only a risk factor.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.75 (Q490), key p.78' },

  { id:'opqb-t11-491', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-glauc',
    stem:'A **50-year-old female** came to the out-patient clinic with **headache, red eye, mid dilated irreactive pupil and corneal haze**. The **first line of treatment** should be:',
    options:['Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors.','Laser iridotomy.','Filtering operation,','Cyclo-cryo-therapy'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"Immediate management of AACG is medical IOP-lowering therapy **(IV acetazolamide, topical beta-blockers),** followed by definitive treatment **(laser iridotomy).**\n\n---\n\n*(The line above is the bank's own printed explanation, transcribed verbatim. The notes below are added for study.)*\n\n**The diagnosis is written into the stem.** `L12) Glaucoma 2 Cong, PACG, 2ry` lists the signs of an acute attack: \"Diminution of vision · IOP → ++++ (stony hard) · **Corneal oedema** — (Fundus examination, gonioscopy → usually not possible) · Ciliary injection · **Mid-dilated vertically oval non-reactive pupil** · Shallow anterior chamber / Iris bombe · Eyelid oedema\", with symptoms of \"Pain → ++++ · Coloured haloes · Diminution of vision → rapid · Redness · Nausea / Vomiting\". Headache, redness, a mid-dilated fixed pupil and corneal haze are four of them.\n\n**Why a carbonic anhydrase inhibitor first.** `L12`'s medical protocol opens with \"Hospitalisation\" and then \"IOP lowering Rx → **Systemic acetazolamide** · Topical beta-blockers · Topical alpha agonists · Topical CAIs · Topical miotics (pilocarpine)\" and \"Hyperosmotic agents: IV mannitol · Oral hypertonic glycerol\". Systemic acetazolamide — a carbonic anhydrase inhibitor — is the first agent the deck names, and the bank's box names it too.\n\n**Why medicine before surgery, when the surgery is the cure.** `L12`'s own parenthesis explains it: with corneal oedema, \"Fundus examination, **gonioscopy → usually not possible**\". A laser iridotomy needs a clear cornea to focus through and a visible iris to aim at, and the surgeon needs to see the angle to know whether an iridotomy will even help. Bringing the pressure down clears the cornea and makes the definitive procedure possible. **The sequence is not optional; it is imposed by the optics of a swollen cornea.**\n\n**Why the other three are wrong — all three are treatments, at the wrong moment.**\n\n| Option | What it is | Why not first |\n|---|---|---|\n| **B. Laser iridotomy** | ⚠️ the **definitive** treatment. `L12`: \"Iridotomy — **Laser (standard)**, Surgical\", and the bank's own box names it as what follows. | cannot be done through an oedematous cornea, and would be aimed at an angle nobody has yet seen. This is the near-miss distractor, and getting it wrong is a sequencing error rather than a knowledge one. |\n| **C. Filtering operation** | `L11`: \"Creation of a fistula between the anterior chamber and the subconjunctival space (trabeculectomy)\". `L12` lists it under surgical treatment. | reserved for an angle permanently sealed by peripheral anterior synechiae — a question that cannot be answered until gonioscopy is possible. Q464 keys exactly that decision. |\n| **D. Cyclo-cryo-therapy** | freezing the ciliary body to destroy aqueous-producing epithelium | a salvage procedure for eyes with poor visual prognosis. This eye's vision is recoverable; destroying its ciliary body would be indefensible. |\n\n**The full algorithm, once, for the five acute vignettes in this topic.** Break the attack medically (Q491) → let the cornea clear → examine the angle (Q490, Q493) → choose the operation from what the angle shows (Q464, Q476) → treat the fellow eye prophylactically (Q449). Q483 keys the topical miotic within the medical stage.",
    objective:'Acute angle closure is first treated medically — systemic acetazolamide and topical pressure-lowering agents — because the definitive laser iridotomy cannot be performed through an oedematous cornea or planned without seeing the angle.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.75 (Q491), key p.78' },

  { id:'opqb-t11-492', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-glauc',
    stem:'During routine IOP measurements, you discovered a patient with an **IOP of 30-mmHg. & 25 mm Hg**. The **CVD ratio was 0.4** and the **field of vision showed paracentral scotoma**. The **angle of AC was open**. On following up this patient, **which test you can rely on most safely**?',
    options:['Deterioration in visual acuity.','Regular checking the IOP to be kept below 20 mmHg.','Regular fundus examination to follow up the progression of cupping.','Regular field plotting to follow up the progression of field changes'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"In early glaucoma with IOP controlled, **visual field progression is the most reliable monitoring method.**\n\n---\n\n*(The line above is the bank's own printed explanation, transcribed verbatim. The notes below are added for study.)*\n\n*(The stem prints \"**CVD ratio**\"; the intended term is the **C/D** — cup:disc — ratio, which the same bank prints correctly as \"C/D\" on Q496 and as \"C: D\" on Q497.)*\n\n**The diagnosis is already made by the stem.** Raised pressure in both eyes, an open angle, and a **paracentral scotoma** — which `L11) Glaucoma Diag, POAG` names first among the field changes, \"Paracentral scotomata in **Bjerrum's area** (10 – 20 degrees of central field)\", and which Q462's printed box calls \"the first to be affected in POAG\". This is early primary open angle glaucoma. The question is not what it is; it is what to watch.\n\n**Why the field.** `L11` states the aim of treatment as \"**preserve visual function** to beyond the patient life expectancy\", so visual function is what must be audited. The field is the earliest measurable expression of that function in this disease, and it is a *measurement* — plotted, filed, and comparable point by point between visits.\n\n**Why the other three are less safe.**\n\n| Option | Why it fails as a monitor |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Deterioration in visual acuity** | ⚠️ the worst answer, and the one that would blind the patient. `L11` confines acuity change to \"Diminution of central vision **in end stage disease**\", and gives the presentation as \"Most commonly → asymptomatic\". Waiting for the Snellen line to drop means waiting until the field has already been destroyed. Q456 keys the same point: \"only central field is important in glaucoma\" is the WRONG statement. |\n| **B. Regular checking the IOP to be kept below 20 mmHg** | pressure is a *risk factor*, not the disease. `L11` is explicit — \"Glaucoma is a specific type of optic neuropathy in which intraocular pressure (IOP) is a **significant risk factor**\" — and names the two states that prove it: \"normal tension glaucoma\" (damage at normal pressure) and \"ocular hypertension\" (raised pressure, no damage). A patient can be at 18 mmHg and still be losing field. A single target number for every eye ignores that. |\n| **C. Regular fundus examination to follow up cupping** | ⚠️ a genuine monitor, and the strong distractor. But it is a subjective judgement of an appearance, and small rim changes are hard to call between visits. Q477's printed box argues this at length: \"disc changes could sometimes happen without affecting the field and also due to the fact that **minor disc changes could be missed**\". |\n\n**On the 0.4.** ⚠️ The cup:disc ratio in this stem sits at a threshold on which this module's sources genuinely disagree. `L11` prints \"Clinically → enlargement of cup/disc ratio > **0.3**\", which makes 0.4 abnormal. The book prints a normal range of **0.2–0.4** with referral above 0.5, which makes it normal. The shipped `pup-10` chapter prints **0.3–0.5** with referral above 0.6. **All three are held; none is corrected.** Nothing here turns on it — the paracentral scotoma and the pressures have already made the diagnosis, and the key is about *monitoring*, not about whether 0.4 is abnormal. Where a figure is needed, this entry uses `L11`'s, and says so.\n\n**Same principle, three more times.** Q456, Q477 and Q497 all key the field over the alternatives.",
    objective:'In established early open angle glaucoma the safest monitor is serial visual field plotting — acuity changes only at end stage, pressure is a risk factor rather than the disease, and disc assessment is subjective.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.75 (Q492), key p.78' },

  { id:'opqb-t11-493', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-glauc',
    stem:'A female patient came with an attack of **severe ocular pain, marked redness, and high IOP**. She received **medical treatment until the attack subsided**. **What is your next step?**',
    options:['Keep on medical treatment.','Subscleral trabeculotomy','Laser iridotomy.','Examine and decide according to the Angle State.'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**The attack is over; now look at the angle, because the angle decides which operation this eye needs.**\n\n`L12) Glaucoma 2 Cong, PACG, 2ry` explains why this step could not have been taken earlier: during the attack, \"Fundus examination, **gonioscopy → usually not possible**\", because the cornea is oedematous. Medical treatment — \"Hospitalisation · Systemic acetazolamide · Topical beta-blockers · Topical alpha agonists · Topical CAIs · Topical miotics (pilocarpine) · IV mannitol · Oral hypertonic glycerol · Topical steroids\" — exists to reach this moment: a quiet eye with a clear cornea in which the angle can finally be seen.\n\n**What gonioscopy is looking for, and what each finding means.** `L11) Glaucoma Diag, POAG` and `L12` define the distinction — \"Open angle → outflow route (trabecular meshwork) **visibly exposed** to the aqueous humour; Closed angle → … **not visibly exposed**\" — and `L1,2) Ocular Anatomy, Symptomatology & Basic Eye Examination` photographs the finding that changes the plan, \"**Peripheral anterior synechiae** … bridging the angle\", on slide 24.\n\n| What the angle shows | What it means | Operation |\n|---|---|---|\n| Narrow but free — the iris was only lying against the meshwork | pure pupil block | **iridotomy** (`L12`: \"Laser (standard), Surgical\") |\n| Sealed by peripheral anterior synechiae over much of its circumference | the drain is permanently gone | **filtering surgery — trabeculectomy** (`L12` lists it; `L11` describes it as a fistula to the subconjunctival space) |\n\n**Why the other three are wrong.**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Keep on medical treatment** | `L12` gives angle closure a **surgical** endpoint in every one of its stages — iridotomy even for the intermittent, symptom-free form. Drops do not deal with a mechanically closable angle, and the continuum's next station is \"Chronic narrow angle glaucoma\" and then \"Absolute glaucoma — **Blind painful eye**\". |\n| **B. Subscleral trabeculotomy** | opening Schlemm's canal from outside. `L12` assigns trabeculotomy to **congenital** glaucoma with an opaque cornea, where the meshwork is dysgenetic. It does nothing about pupil block or synechiae. |\n| **C. Laser iridotomy** | ⚠️ the near-miss, and probably the right operation in most such eyes — but the question is what to do *next*, and choosing an operation before examining the angle is the error being tested. An iridotomy in an angle already welded shut by synechiae relieves nothing. |\n\n**⚠️ Read alongside Q491, which looks contradictory and is not.** Q491's printed box says medical therapy is \"followed by definitive treatment (**laser iridotomy**)\" — which would seem to make option C right here. The two keys are consistent: Q491 asks what treatment *follows* the medical stage in general terms, and Q493 asks what the *next step* is, which is the examination that establishes whether the iridotomy will work. **Neither key is disputed.** Q464 states the same rule as a surgical choice and Q476 as a management step.\n\n**The instruction the topic repeats four times.** Q464, Q476, Q490 and Q493 all reduce to one sentence: **look at the angle before you decide.**\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'After an acute angle closure attack has been broken medically the next step is gonioscopy, because whether the angle is merely narrow or sealed by peripheral anterior synechiae determines whether iridotomy or filtering surgery is needed.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.76 (Q493), key p.78' },

  { id:'opqb-t11-494', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-glauc',
    stem:'On examination of a baby **6-month-old**, the right eye was of average size. The **left eye was larger in size, with increased corneal diameter and corneal edema**. **Fundus examination was not possible**. The **first indicated investigation** is:',
    options:['Measurement of visual acuity with preferential looking.','Electroretinogram to assess the retinal function.','Visual evoked response to assess visual acuity.','Ultrasonography to exclude retinoblastoma'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**With the fundus invisible, the first investigation must be one that sees past an opaque cornea — and that is ultrasound.**\n\n`L12) Glaucoma 2 Cong, PACG, 2ry` gives the signs of congenital glaucoma: \"**Corneal oedema**, Haab's stria · **Enlarged corneal diameter** · Broad limbus · Increased IOP · Optic nerve cupping · **Increased axial length (A-scan)**\", with the mechanism \"Pathology → Elevated IOP → **enlargement of the globe → increased corneal diameter, broad limbus, increased antero-posterior diameter of the eye** · aqueous entry into corneal stroma → corneal oedema · optic nerve cupping\". The stem gives three of those signs in one eye.\n\n**Why the answer is not merely diagnostic.** The stem says the fundus cannot be seen. That is the emergency. A unilaterally enlarged infant eye with an unviewable posterior segment leaves one question that must be answered before anything else: **is there a tumour behind the cloudy cornea?** Retinoblastoma is the malignancy that kills children who present with an eye that looks wrong, and it can raise pressure and enlarge a globe. Ultrasound passes through an opaque cornea, images the posterior segment, and detects the calcified intraocular mass. Nothing else on the list can look behind the cornea at all.\n\n**⚠️ The key's rationale and the deck's rationale differ, and both are recorded.** `L12` gives ultrasound to congenital glaucoma for a different purpose — \"**Increased axial length (A-scan)**\", i.e. measuring the stretched globe as a sign of the disease. The bank frames the same investigation as tumour exclusion. The two readings agree on the *investigation* and differ on *why*; that retinoblastoma must be excluded in an infant eye with an obscured fundus is **not stated in `L12`**, whose differential diagnosis lists \"Traumatic corneal oedema from forceps delivery · Corneal ulcer · Congenital corneal opacification · Congenital high myopia · Congenital nasolacrimal duct obstruction · Congenital optic nerve pit\" — six conditions, none of them a tumour. **The key is not disputed**; it is right for both reasons.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong — and all three fail for the same reason.**\n\n| Option | What it measures | Why not first |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A. Preferential looking acuity** | how well the child sees | a behavioural test that will be poor in this eye and will tell you nothing about why. It changes no decision. |\n| **B. Electroretinogram** | retinal photoreceptor function | tests function, not structure. It cannot show a mass, and retinal function is not the question — the stem describes a corneal and globe problem. |\n| **C. Visual evoked response** | conduction along the visual pathway | same objection. It reports the state of a pathway whose front end you have not yet been able to look at. |\n\n**What follows the ultrasound.** `L12` treats congenital glaucoma surgically — \"Medical → temporary till surgery\"; \"Surgical → **Clear cornea → goniotomy**; **Opaque cornea →** Trabeculotomy · Trabeculectomy with antimetabolites · Synthetic valve implantation\". This baby's cornea is oedematous, which puts the operation in the second row. Q460 keys goniotomy for the clear-cornea case, and Q452 is the same disease at one month old.\n\n*(Secondary chapter: `op-white` — retinoblastoma and the investigation of an abnormal infant eye are taught there and in `L24) Ocular tumors`.)*\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'A unilaterally enlarged infant eye with an oedematous cornea and an unviewable fundus needs ultrasonography first — it images through an opaque cornea, excludes retinoblastoma, and measures the increased axial length of congenital glaucoma.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.76 (Q494), key p.78' },

  { id:'opqb-t11-495', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-glauc',
    stem:'A patient presenting with **mild cataract in one eye** in which **cup/disc ratio is 0.8**, while he is giving history with **painless loss of [vision] in the other eye few years ago**, and his intraocular pressures are **right eye 28 mmHg left eye 30 mmHg**: what is the diagnosis?',
    options:['Primary open angle glaucoma','Normal tension glaucoma','Phacolytic glaucoma','Phacomorphic glaucoma'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**Bilateral raised pressure, gross cupping, and a painless loss of the fellow eye years ago is primary open angle glaucoma; the cataract is a bystander.**\n\n*(⚠️ **Source misprint.** The bank prints \"history with **painless loss of in the other eye** few years ago\" — the noun is missing. Read as *painless loss of vision*. The printed fragment is quoted here rather than silently corrected.)*\n\n**Every element of the stem points the same way.**\n\n| Finding | What it means in `L11) Glaucoma Diag, POAG` |\n|---|---|\n| **C/D 0.8** | far past the deck's threshold: \"Clinically → enlargement of cup/disc ratio **> 0.3**\", with \"Total cupping with nasal shift of blood vessels\" as the end point. 0.8 is unambiguous on every reading in this module — the book's, `pup-10`'s and `L11`'s alike. |\n| **IOP 28 and 30, both eyes** | bilateral, chronic elevation. `L11`'s pathology: \"Increased resistance to Aqueous humour drainage → trabecular meshwork → Schlemm's canal → collector channels\" |\n| **Painless** loss, **years** ago | `L11`'s presentation: \"Most commonly → **asymptomatic** … Advanced disease → peripheral visual field loss, diminution of vision\". No pain at any stage. |\n| **The other eye already lost** | POAG is bilateral, and asymmetric. One eye reaching end stage while the other is still salvageable is the classic and tragic presentation. |\n| **Mild cataract** | a distractor. \"Mild\" means it is not the cause of anything. |\n\n**Why the other three are wrong.**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **B. Normal tension glaucoma** | ⚠️ `L11` defines it as an **exception** to the triad — \"eyes with optic neuropathy and **normal IOP**\". This patient's pressures are 28 and 30. The name excludes itself. |\n| **C. Phacolytic glaucoma** | `L17) Cataract I` and `L18) Cataract II` require a **hypermature** cataract whose degenerate capsule leaks protein — \"Phacolytic Glaucoma — Hypermature/mature — Liquefied cortex leaks proteins — **HM, pain, redness**\". The stem says *mild* cataract and *painless*. Both halves fail. Q466 and Q489 cover this entity. |\n| **D. Phacomorphic glaucoma** | `L17`: \"Intumescence (lens fibres fluid imbibition) → increased lens thickness → **pupil block** → elevated IOP\", and `L18`: \"Immature cataract — Swollen lens → **angle closure** — Red eye + pain + HM\". Acute, painful, unilateral and angle-closing. Nothing in the stem matches. Q463 and Q478 cover it. |\n\n**The trap the question is built on.** A cataract is mentioned first, so the two lens-induced glaucomas look inviting; and the pressures are high enough that a candidate primed on angle closure may reach for the acute answer. The discriminators are *painless*, *years*, *bilateral pressures*, and *mild*. Lens-induced glaucoma is an acute, unilateral, painful emergency arising from a lens that is anything but mild.\n\n**The clinical lesson.** `L11` opens its treatment slide with \"Glaucoma is a **potentially blinding disease**, referral to an ophthalmologist is a must\". This patient has already lost one eye to it without ever having a symptom that brought him in. The remaining eye is at 28 mmHg with a 0.8 cup — Q477 keys a falling field as the indication to escalate to surgery, and Q492 keys serial perimetry as the way to detect it.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Bilaterally raised pressure with a 0.8 cup:disc ratio and painless loss of the fellow eye years earlier is primary open angle glaucoma; a mild cataract is incidental, and the lens-induced glaucomas are acute, painful and unilateral.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.76 (Q495), key p.78' },

  { id:'opqb-t11-496', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-glauc',
    stem:'A **89-year-old** patient, **cardiac and hypertensive**, was found to have an **IOP of 36** on routine ophthalmological exam with a **C/D ratio of 0.8**. Which of the following is **TRUE**?',
    options:['This patient will mostly have visual field defects','He definitely has a vision worse than 20/40','Urgent treatment with an IOP lowering agent is mandatory','A and C','Band C'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**A cup:disc ratio of 0.8 at 36 mmHg is advanced, established glaucoma — so field loss is near certain and treatment cannot wait — but central acuity may still be perfectly good.**\n\n*(Five options. ⚠️ Option E is printed \"**Band C**\", with \"B and C\" set solid; read as *B and C*. The printed text is kept as it appears.)*\n\n**Why A is true.** `L11) Glaucoma Diag, POAG` ties structure to function directly: \"**Neural rim thinning → functional changes** · Disturbance of visual function → Visual field changes → Paracentral scotomata in Bjerrum's area · Arcuate scotomata · Nasal step · Ring scotoma · Constriction of visual field (→ tubular field) · Temporal island of vision\". A 0.8 cup means most of the neuroretinal rim has already gone. Rim tissue *is* the axons that carry the field; when four-fifths of the disc is cup, the field cannot be intact. Note the wording — \"will **mostly** have\" — a probability, which is exactly right.\n\n**Why C is true.** `L11` calls glaucoma \"a potentially blinding disease\" for which \"referral to an ophthalmologist is a **must**\". 36 mmHg is roughly double the upper limit of normal, in an eye that has already demonstrated it cannot tolerate pressure. Every further week at that level costs axons that do not grow back.\n\n**Why B is false — and this is the whole question.** `L11` confines acuity loss to one line: \"Visual acuity changes → Diminution of central vision **in end stage disease**\". Q462's printed box says the same thing about the field: \"**Fixation (central vision) may be spared until the disease is markedly advanced.**\" A patient can read 20/20 through a field reduced to a tube, because the few degrees around fixation are the last to go. The word that kills option B is \"**definitely**\": nothing about a cup:disc ratio predicts a Snellen line.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Will mostly have visual field defects** | **TRUE** — a 0.8 cup means the rim, and therefore the field, is largely gone |\n| **B. Definitely has vision worse than 20/40** | **FALSE** — central acuity is spared until end stage |\n| **C. Urgent IOP lowering is mandatory** | **TRUE** — 36 mmHg on an already-damaged nerve |\n| **D. A and C** | **the key** |\n| **E. \"Band C\" (B and C)** | false, because B is false |\n\n**Why the age and the comorbidities are in the stem.** They are there to make you hesitate over C — is it worth treating an 89-year-old? — and to complicate *how*. `L11` sets the aim of treatment as \"preserve visual function **to beyond the patient life expectancy** at an affordable cost with the least side effects\", which is the only place in either deck where life expectancy enters the calculation. It argues for choosing a gentler route in a frail patient, not for withholding treatment: an eye at 36 mmHg with a 0.8 cup can go blind within the patient's remaining years. ⚠️ The specific caution that topical beta-blockers are systemically absorbed and hazardous in cardiac disease, which is what \"cardiac and hypertensive\" is hinting at, is **not taken from the course material** — `L11` and `L12` list the drug classes without contraindications.\n\n**Q497 is this question's deliberate opposite** — same routine screening setting, but a C/D of 0.4, an IOP of 26 and normal fundi, where the key is to wait for the fields.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'A 0.8 cup:disc ratio at 36 mmHg means the rim and therefore the field are already largely lost and pressure lowering is urgent — but central acuity is spared until end stage, so a specific Snellen level cannot be predicted.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.76 (Q496), key p.78' },

  { id:'opqb-t11-497', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-glauc',
    stem:"A **54-year-old male** patient with **negative past medical and ocular histories** was found to have an **IOP of 26** on routine eye examination. His **C: D ratio is 0.4 OU**, vision **20/30 OU** and he has **normal fundi**. **What's the next step** in the management of this patient?",
    options:['He should be started on topical beta blockers immediately to lower the IOP','A visual fields examination must be ordered first','He should be started on topical beta blockers while awaiting the results of the visual fields','He should be given mannitol or IV acetazolamide before discharging him from the clinic','Cand D'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"IOP 26 mmHg with normal optic disc → **suspect ocular hypertension; confirm functional loss with visual fields before starting treatment.**\n\n---\n\n*(The line above is the bank's own printed explanation, transcribed verbatim. The notes below are added for study.)*\n\n*(Five options. ⚠️ Option E is printed \"**Cand D**\", with \"C and D\" set solid; read as *C and D*. The printed text is kept as it appears.)*\n\n**Why this is not yet glaucoma.** `L11) Glaucoma Diag, POAG` names the state exactly: among the exceptions to the classical triad it lists \"eyes with **normal optic nerves and elevated IOP** (**ocular hypertension**)\". That is this patient — pressure up, disc normal, fundi normal, no history. `L11` is emphatic that the pressure is not the disease: \"Glaucoma is a specific type of optic neuropathy in which intraocular pressure (IOP) is a **significant risk factor**.\"\n\n**Why the field comes before the drug.** The triad `L11` opens with is \"Elevated intraocular pressure (IOP) · Optic nerve affection (cupping) · **Visual field defects**\". Two of the three have been checked and one has not. Treating now would commit a healthy 54-year-old to daily drops for the rest of his life on the strength of a single number, and — worse — would destroy the untreated baseline against which every future field is compared. A field plotted before treatment is the reference for the next thirty years.\n\n**Why the other four are wrong.**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Beta blockers immediately** | treats a risk factor as though it were a diagnosis, and loses the baseline. |\n| **C. Beta blockers while awaiting the fields** | ⚠️ the subtle one, and it fails for the same reason: **a field plotted on treatment is not a baseline.** If it comes back normal you will never know whether it was normal all along. Starting a drug the same day removes the very information you ordered the test to get. |\n| **D. Mannitol or IV acetazolamide before discharge** | ⚠️ emergency agents. `L12) Glaucoma 2 Cong, PACG, 2ry` lists them under the **acute attack**: \"Systemic acetazolamide\" and \"Hyperosmotic agents: IV mannitol, Oral hypertonic glycerol\", alongside \"Hospitalisation\". This man has a white eye, 20/30 vision and no symptoms. Q484 keys mannitol as the *strongest* pressure-lowering agent — and this question is where that fact meets its limit: **strongest is not most appropriate.** |\n| **E. \"Cand D\" (C and D)** | compounds two wrong answers. |\n\n**On the 0.4.** ⚠️ This module's three sources disagree about whether 0.4 is normal. `L11` prints \"enlargement of cup/disc ratio > **0.3**\", which would make it abnormal; the book prints a normal range of **0.2–0.4** with referral above 0.5, which makes it normal; the shipped `pup-10` chapter prints **0.3–0.5** with referral above 0.6. All three are held and none is corrected. Nothing here depends on it — the stem itself supplies the finding that settles the matter, \"**normal fundi**\", and the ratio is the same in both eyes, which removes the asymmetry that would otherwise be suspicious. Where a figure is quoted above it is `L11`'s.\n\n**Q496 is the deliberate counterpart**, same screening setting with a C/D of 0.8 and an IOP of 36, where urgent treatment *is* mandatory. The pair teaches one rule: **the disc and the field decide whether to treat; the pressure alone never does.** Q477 and Q492 key the field over the alternatives in patients already on treatment.",
    objective:'Raised pressure with a normal disc and fundus is ocular hypertension, not glaucoma — the visual field must be plotted before treatment starts, both to look for functional loss and to preserve an untreated baseline.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.76 (Q497), key p.78' }
